#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1200.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134695} :activity/id #uuid "93a7b58c-f55f-5517-ad99-318bb8790e55" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048284 :topic/id #uuid "90a1bf39-5b4c-5a6f-82d9-c3528eb17b2c" :topic/math-academy-id 2166} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134695 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135059 :tutorial/content "Let's consider the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} &=-2 \\\\ -{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2} &=4 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2} &=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nIt is easy to show that the system is inconsistent (i.e., it has no solution). However, in real-world problems, we might want to find an approximate solution to the system. \n\nLet's formulate the problem a little more rigorously. Firstly, we write down the system in the form $A\\mathbf x = \\mathbf b\\mathbin{:}$\n![](images/22/22b8abd4638e579875755f482442cf1dbf89653bd9d396b4a802217e25177839.svg)\n\nWe are interested in vectors $\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}$ such that $A\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}$ is very close to $\\mathbf b.$ More precisely, we wish to *minimize the distance* between $A\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}$ and $\\mathbf b.$ Recall that the distance between $A\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}$ and $\\mathbf b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Vert  A{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}} - \\mathbf{b} \\Vert.\n$$\n\nThe smaller the value of $\\Vert  A{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}} - \\mathbf{b} \\Vert,$ the better our approximation $\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}$ will be. \n\nIn terms of vector subspaces, we wish to find a vector $A{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}}$ that lies in $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ and is closest to $\\mathbf{b}.$\nTherefore, $A{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}}$ must be the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{b}$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of $A.$\n\n![](images/3a/3a1c79036954f0e04212892b7e3d79c75f8cd7b81d7785441f090f89a4eb6faf.png)\n\nIt can be shown that ${\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}}$ is found by solving the corresponding **normal equation**, given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\hat{x} & ={A}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe'll prove this at the end of the lesson. Note that the normal equation is obtained by left-multiplying the equation $A {\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}} = \\mathbf b$ by $A^T.$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix $A^T \\! A$ is invertible. Therefore, left-multiplying the normal equation by $(A^T \\! A)^{-1},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}} = (A^T \\! A)^{-1}A^T \\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the right-hand side as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -4 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 18\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -20\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{99}\\begin{bmatrix}18 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -20\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{8}{33} \\\\ -\\frac{38}{33}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vector ${\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}}$ is called the **least-squares solution** of the given system.\n\n**Watch out!** We assumed that the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, which guarantees that $A^T \\! A\\,$ is invertible. However, if the columns of $A$ are linearly *dependent,* then we have to use a different approach to solve the problem." :tutorial/id #uuid "548ecdec-2401-51e1-b2cf-d63f0b5a595d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3636 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "c3719452-213a-5a8f-92fa-ac5912c1a8ba" :step/math-academy-id 20269 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134697}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134696 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133799 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133608 :question/id #uuid "e7fb27f0-67b3-58fa-a582-acd4a4ebe045" :question/math-academy-id "e-5588"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74efec6b-6e5f-5761-a116-5c94e3fb4326" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049151} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049548} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049779}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134363} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134364} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326908} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326909} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366139} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366504} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382559} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388799} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392422} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394668} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394717} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482611}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System"} :step/id #uuid "eb5011df-f314-5469-89c6-7bc2e1633d5b" :step/math-academy-id 20270 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134778}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134697 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133800 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133609 :question/id #uuid "9f8a0e56-d0c5-56dd-9204-c13922ec8ee4" :question/math-academy-id "e-5589"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d23c4292-6fdd-5cba-a2e7-0f4d770b2309" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049970}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134329} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134330} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326888} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354438} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354439} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392402} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394710} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482514} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482549} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482605}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System Given in Matrix Form"} :step/id #uuid "bd48fc68-e028-5256-934f-98b803b1f3f4" :step/math-academy-id 20271 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134696}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134778 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133848 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133663 :question/id #uuid "af2b3f92-fed5-5a50-b662-eaadd9b7adbc" :question/math-academy-id "e-7429"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5924037a-ddc3-5f50-b4b0-1bd813cef255" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048506}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134365} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134366} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354408} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354409} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354410} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354440} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365066} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366497} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382566} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382573} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482586} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500322}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Distance Between a Vector to a Subspace of Solutions of a Linear System"} :step/id #uuid "e9d074bb-35df-566a-80ad-6c15d1e9fe5f" :step/math-academy-id 27532 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134892}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134892 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135146 :tutorial/content "Suppose that ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) is an ![](images/67/677276828b5438bcf8d73facec02bc738e2e4f02bb6c5ff83bd5ae41902dd8ea.svg) matrix with linearly independent columns. We'll now explain why the least-squares solution ![](images/a4/a4d10ece37c4369a97f57734306d77d35e94a240b68f2625b4d45791bdc13378.svg) to the least-squares problem \n\n![](images/f7/f72b4cb4f9fd00c6a409ed62ae97aad9915179065d14609810459cbd7b5b63c2.svg)\n\ncan be found by solving the normal equation\n![](images/dc/dcd7a5cf3452038ee326ed1b4a5ab2913f41ad408849ddda132cbdfee7c35887.svg)\n\nThe vector ![](images/eb/eba4e0fbcf4207b493c3a3a4eecc12df848d9d995708881cce8542ae5d04bdd9.svg) must be the orthogonal projection of ![](images/63/639196a1a30d6f6280e3cf491e6cf3c2b0b991892970f7b641a9b300defb034f.svg) onto the subspace spanned by the columns of ![](images/c8/c8c72090d28b539bed2177052b06540383d63ac720008f1b301dafcac4fa6227.svg)\n\n![](images/02/024c12b780df0414ab1cf012e5733603fb1167c9533e199c46628d73ad12ebd9.png)\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n![](images/c3/c3f058a925aef4913b39914985d02cfb3d7b2752c0116aa43b2e73d804fa29d4.svg) \n\nwhich is equivalent to saying that ![](images/c5/c54ecdc03925384b80bdbddc9df105fb34d4b31426570aa7c6446815c6a90ed9.svg) is orthogonal to each column ![](images/16/162e4a7398d3a8c47cc967b78bf43167256270c609bfdc271e46a2ea32ade145.svg) of ![](images/c8/c8c72090d28b539bed2177052b06540383d63ac720008f1b301dafcac4fa6227.svg) Thus, the dot product of the columns of ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) and the vector ![](images/c5/c54ecdc03925384b80bdbddc9df105fb34d4b31426570aa7c6446815c6a90ed9.svg) must be equal to ![](images/37/37dd4bc8064071b5d4219d47ac24847861bdbe0c82b48e4997c5d7d8b4a3d566.svg)\n\n![](images/57/5701e3d1325d0ec137b7d405796114e3044f5076c11fa9e2e10fe70d16919a88.svg)\n\nThis system of equations can be written in terms of matrix products as\n\n![](images/92/921a6627dff46463fc16fc80fe16353fb80942f08c5c632d6665920be96676ed.svg) \n\nFinally, expanding the left-hand side, we obtain our normal equation:\n\n![](images/86/867c103511eeb1e208028ea1a3af3eb4b397c50a3131efea683b55d959d4ec4e.svg)" :tutorial/id #uuid "43d14ff9-b350-5406-9f4b-64096d8f3215" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6154 :tutorial/title "Deriving the Normal Equation"} :step/id #uuid "df64bb85-56ef-5ee5-80d4-956e1d949a18" :step/math-academy-id 37337}] :activity/title "The Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System (Without Collinearity)" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186134026}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500387} :activity/id #uuid "4afc5eb0-b00c-5311-ad64-1f529d40ac87" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048284 :topic/id #uuid "90a1bf39-5b4c-5a6f-82d9-c3528eb17b2c" :topic/math-academy-id 2166} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500387 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326908 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "125e5ea0-2d09-53c7-bf5a-6db86f01788b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{11}{20} \\\\ -\\frac{7}{20}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324136} {:answer/id #uuid "16df845b-8aca-58e3-ae92-9d86f331bce3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{13}{20} \\\\ \\frac{1}{20}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324173} {:answer/id #uuid "24d2ae87-e1f9-50af-9aa6-98e8929a2d33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{17}{20} \\\\ \\frac{3}{20}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324274} {:answer/id #uuid "b8e4ad4a-f6d5-57ac-9b52-100c6666021e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{20} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{20}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325558} {:answer/id #uuid "cf6ec109-3e58-5c22-b7fa-223132d14aab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{20} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325773}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325558} :answer-field/id #uuid "24dd5431-2243-5962-ae01-cbb9b9be0bfc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326282}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "082f22b9-7dbc-5c7c-88d0-775a3d957e87" :question/math-academy-id "q-41776" :question/problem "Find the least-squares solution to the system of linear equations given below. You're given that the columns of the corresponding coefficient matrix are linearly independent.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} &=1 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} &=-1 \\\\ -{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\hat{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\hat{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 14\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{40}\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{20} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{20}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "0c175108-3251-5f6f-a837-933e8a149a06" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500431}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500391 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500322 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13c9ab58-bd09-5df1-8d1d-2d4ce831e5f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499698} {:answer/id #uuid "4fda416c-29ab-5041-9567-21fe9188edc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499807} {:answer/id #uuid "6123a26d-2828-5a47-a292-99e6c3705513" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499845} {:answer/id #uuid "bcbbd696-cd17-5e43-a03c-c815bdcc9a15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500039} {:answer/id #uuid "c898ab35-05fd-5fa1-8c7c-3ba6a9711cb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500064}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499845} :answer-field/id #uuid "112e9326-3d57-5ecf-afb8-b6f921f35c4c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500187}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "7b37b44d-e7cb-5e9e-b8af-e9f1e4941b43" :question/math-academy-id "q-140953" :question/problem "Consider the system $Ax=b$, where the matrix $A$ and the vector $b$ are shown below. Given that the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, find the minimum value of $‖Ax-b‖.$\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}}} \\\\ Ax & =b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\hat{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\hat{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\,\\,\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 7\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{45}\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe minimum value of $‖Ax-b‖$ is attained when $x=\\hat{x},$ which is the least-squares solution of the system $Ax=b.$ In this case, $A\\hat{x}$ represents the orthogonal projection of $b$ onto the column space of $A,$ and $‖A\\hat{x}-b‖$ is the distance between the vector $b$ and the column space. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\hat{x}-b & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n‖A\\hat{x}-b‖=\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}}=\\sqrt{3}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "1daadfd6-2782-5d18-911b-b764bf042f15"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500431 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354410 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59881df0-2ac6-532e-8fb9-91b698aa746f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352356} {:answer/id #uuid "6136a078-6ec6-579a-809a-d2077533b6a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352433} {:answer/id #uuid "76500562-20f2-5c7e-bdb3-4992adaf7d17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{111}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352607} {:answer/id #uuid "7917c258-2cdc-5cb9-a21d-ebdf986ccad6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{101}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352623} {:answer/id #uuid "7cb29578-9f41-5fba-9274-bad7f34afe91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{110}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352661}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186352661} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e227d03-ecbf-552f-86b6-2f39084c0aba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354049}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "22b88813-fc85-5da7-b050-31934e04cdb3" :question/math-academy-id "q-85284" :question/problem "Consider the system $Ax=b$, where the matrix $A$ and the vector $b$ are shown below. Given that the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, find the minimum value of $‖Ax-b‖.$\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 14 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 14 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}} \\\\ Ax & =b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\hat{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\hat{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\,\\,\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 14 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}15 \\\\ 15\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{15}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}15 \\\\ 15\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe minimum value of $‖Ax-b‖$ is attained when $x=\\hat{x},$ which is the least-squares solution of the system $Ax=b.$ In this case, $A\\hat{x}$ represents the orthogonal projection of $b$ onto the column space of $A,$ and $‖A\\hat{x}-b‖$ is the distance between the vector $b$ and the column space. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\hat{x}-b & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 14 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n‖A\\hat{x}-b‖=\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+(-4)^{2}+(-8)^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}=\\sqrt{110}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "a561b4d8-a24f-5ea9-9176-71cdc5599a2d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500445}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500445 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354439 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47f733f4-12f3-5c07-8f4b-304b44459b9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352230} {:answer/id #uuid "8e2c331c-8cc2-5e5e-b4b6-de1a54f34fe1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352806} {:answer/id #uuid "c7ad2f12-4695-596d-8cec-b7f38fb28a29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353297} {:answer/id #uuid "d2a56cfe-ee7f-5cca-9b65-eb74bde205f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353390} {:answer/id #uuid "e9c8975d-a61b-537d-897c-5fdec1b16da4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353601}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186353390} :answer-field/id #uuid "cda82a11-1d36-50fa-8ecc-f1dd1a7dc6ee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354171}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "48fc2839-660f-57b5-b91c-b2fcd5bae103" :question/math-academy-id "q-85899" :question/problem "Given that the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, find the least-squares solution of $Ax=b$, where \n\n$\\,$ $A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -4 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -4 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\hat{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\hat{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={(\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -2 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -4 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & -2\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -2 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}16 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 32 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 24\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}48 \\\\ 48 \\\\ 24\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{96}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}48 \\\\ 48 \\\\ 24\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "d22a3088-3980-554b-950c-e1334497ac66" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500452}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500452 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382559 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5dc9412d-db1d-5f0b-bf46-1547fc9bc9d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382561} {:answer/id #uuid "f6ab9fcd-0d01-5a7c-a562-b332d1fa753b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382562} {:answer/id #uuid "7a397f58-508e-5d63-aeae-854fad3a6043" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382563} {:answer/id #uuid "2756ce9d-d692-57b4-88bb-5f748720577f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382564} {:answer/id #uuid "3f5b1550-1387-5406-a483-f580bc72d844" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382565}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382563} :answer-field/id #uuid "38cb7faf-1c5d-5786-81e1-1833c5631b56" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382560}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "cbf886ad-eced-5102-99df-0e48cd09970a" :question/math-academy-id "q-85905" :question/problem "Find the least-squares solution to the system of linear equations given below. You're given that the columns of the corresponding coefficient matrix are linearly independent.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2} &=8 \\\\ 4{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3} &=2 \\\\ -4{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} &=2 \\\\ 4{x}_{1}-4{x}_{3} &=-6\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}}}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\hat{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\hat{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={(\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & 0 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 2 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & 0 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 2 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}36 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 36 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 36\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -36 \\\\ 36\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{36}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -36 \\\\ 36\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "efc7b297-2494-521d-8ca9-fb8275cf05d1" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500391}}] :activity/title "The Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System (Without Collinearity)" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499645}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 660.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167545} :activity/id #uuid "47275b7c-63ae-58c0-be44-52a56b12a6d5" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048750 :topic/id #uuid "bada0387-801f-5095-946a-2f79c55b271e" :topic/math-academy-id 3270} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167545 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168156 :tutorial/content "When performing certain types of statistical tests (e.g., hypothesis testing), we often need to use standard cumulative distribution function (CDF) tables to carry out the test. \n\nIn this lesson, we'll learn how to construct CDF tables for the binomial distribution and use them to calculate probabilities. \n\nFirst, recall that the probability mass function of the binomial distribution $B(n,p)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\binom{n}{x} \\, p^x (1-p)^{n-x}, \\qquad x=0,1,2,\\ldots, n\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success.\n\nAlso, recall that the CDF of a discrete random variable $X$ is a function $F(x)$ such that\n\n$$\nF(x) = P(X \\leq x).\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's compute the value of $F(x)$ for the distribution $B(5,0.37)$ at $x=0$ and $x=1.$  \n\nTo do that, we use the definition of the cumulative distribution function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(0) & =P(X\\le 0) \\\\  & =P(X=0) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{0})(0.37)^{0}(1-0.37)^{5-0} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (0.37)^{0}\\cdot (0.63)^{5} \\\\  & ≈0.0992 \\\\ F(1) & =P(X\\le 1) \\\\  & =P(X=0)+P(X=1) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{0})(0.37)^{0}(1-0.37)^{5-0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{1})(0.37)^{1}(1-0.37)^{5-1} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (0.37)^{0}\\cdot (0.63)^{5}+5\\cdot (0.37)^{1}\\cdot (0.63)^{4} \\\\  & ≈0.3907.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe CDF data is usually presented as a table. If we were to continue this process and calculate $F(2), F(3), F(4),$ and $F(5),$ our table would look as follows:\n\n| $\\quad x \\quad$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $P(X\\leq x)$ | $\\,\\bbox[gainsboro, 3pt]{\\color{blue}0.0992}\\,$ | $\\,\\bbox[gainsboro, 3pt]{\\color{blue}0.3907}\\,$ | $\\,0.7330\\,$ | $\\,0.9340\\,$ | $\\,0.9931\\,$ | $\\,1.0000\\,$ |\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $F(x)$ is an increasing function.\n\n- Since $n$ is the largest possible value of the binomial distribution, we have $F(n) = 1.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "0d918e93-fef5-5c82-9f8d-6d6e7811ffe1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9442 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "1e620940-e97d-5c6f-b482-486949e38af6" :step/math-academy-id 181471 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167546}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167546 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166726 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166494 :question/id #uuid "56d68682-0e4d-57e5-9441-997cf4d33f29" :question/math-academy-id "e-15052"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ccfebb3-3017-5639-8b67-058c304c9df8" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050256} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050651}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167140} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167141} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425542}] :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Binomial CDF Table"} :step/id #uuid "5c56d010-1578-5a5a-a0db-b758684ff62c" :step/math-academy-id 181472 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167547}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167547 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166727 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166495 :question/id #uuid "92a73e93-f882-5329-a19c-00a6a270bced" :question/math-academy-id "e-15054"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "69c62fd8-05af-57dc-a96c-4dcba884ea32" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167142} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167147} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425543} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425546}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Binomial Probability Using the CDF"} :step/id #uuid "7ba808e1-680b-56a0-b552-ba47fdc7092d" :step/math-academy-id 181475 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167548}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167548 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166728 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166496 :question/id #uuid "c80fb077-2d31-522b-8083-5a72613f84de" :question/math-academy-id "e-15055"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0f0c914d-f2c0-5641-9818-312eac10d1b4" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167143} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167144} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425540} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425544}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Binomial Probability Over an Interval Using the CDF"} :step/id #uuid "ff57f32e-cc93-5cd0-9cf3-218ccc361380" :step/math-academy-id 181478 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167549}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167549 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166729 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166497 :question/id #uuid "c9a6a708-925d-54aa-ac0a-023e5259d19a" :question/math-academy-id "e-15056"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "95b5e3bd-0e7e-56df-80d5-be39de76da16" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167145} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167146} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425541} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425545}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Binomial Probability Over an Interval Containing a \"Greater Than\" Symbol"} :step/id #uuid "479816c5-93e8-5594-9323-95e7f8aa4b3a" :step/math-academy-id 181481}] :activity/title "The CDF of the Binomial Distribution" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186167034}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 1080.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125361} :activity/id #uuid "e6e3df9f-205d-5b79-a182-6a881367dca2" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048769 :topic/id #uuid "bd333eb5-72e6-5eaa-be12-c6bf36306b90" :topic/math-academy-id 1910} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125361 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125768 :tutorial/content "Suppose that $\\mathbf x \\in V$ for some vector space $V,$ and that $\\mathcal B$ and $\\mathcal C$ are two bases of $V.$ \n\nTo find the coordinates of $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to $\\mathcal{C}$ given its coordinates relative to $\\mathcal B,$ we can use the **change-of-basis formula**\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [\\,x\\,]_{B},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we recall that\n\n- $[\\,\\mathbf x\\,]_{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}}$ and $[\\,\\mathbf x\\,]_{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}$ are the coordinates of $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the bases $\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}$ and ${\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}},$ respectively, and\n\n- $P_{\\small{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}$ to ${\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}},$ whose columns are the coordinates of vectors of the basis $\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}$ relative to the basis ${\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}.$\n\nAs an example, suppose we are given two bases $\\mathcal{B}=\\left\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1, \\mathbf{b}_2 \\right\\}$ and $\\mathcal{C}=\\left\\{ \\mathbf{c}_1, \\mathbf{c}_2 \\right\\}$ of a vector space $V,$ and we already know that the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{C}$ is given by\n![](images/d9/d9388f46f84cc0c6b1d7e5cf43ee973085366ae4f44cfe74515c59bfc1994662.svg)\n\nLet $\\mathbf{v} \\in V$ and ![](images/f9/f99f37621c20057077eb7c6e23690a45159cfe1b665c6b88b4a604f8687728bf.svg) Then we can easily find $[\\,\\mathbf{v}\\,]_{\\mathcal{C}},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,v\\,]_{C} & =\\overset{{P}_{B\\to C}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot \\overset{[v]_{B}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "827e9581-9fde-5941-ad91-2c364796e5b1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3217 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "910841a1-0516-546a-8aac-f188c965b763" :step/math-academy-id 17537 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125500}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125362 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124495 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124308 :question/id #uuid "922b0f79-2c87-56f9-bf8a-18711a2a09d7" :question/math-academy-id "e-4838"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e2a1713-45a0-5ac9-812e-efe047dbc9eb" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050008} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050825}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124968} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124991} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247372} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247373} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323887} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394394} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394435} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417186} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417187} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417190} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440462} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440466}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector in a New Basis Given the Change-of-Coordinates Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "ad493885-054b-5d5e-a186-80a2c3801b8b" :step/math-academy-id 17538 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125363}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125363 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124496 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124309 :question/id #uuid "a3911ca2-a357-5f37-8abc-e01e5518f24b" :question/math-academy-id "e-4840"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ee52fd3-6c79-5016-ade5-46e7bfbb557a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049548}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124994} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125034} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323807} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351438} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351452} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386696} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394442} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417185} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417192} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440464} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440465} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440469} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486427} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486444}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector in a New Basis Given the Inverse Change-of-Coordinates Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "45243fec-9ed2-54d8-b6ef-0d2c0a1e7087" :step/math-academy-id 17541 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125364}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125364 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124497 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124310 :question/id #uuid "a2d3629f-5b7c-5b65-998f-8754ff11e7b7" :question/math-academy-id "e-4841"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f58bd46-07f7-54a5-8499-5e4e3515b610" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124993} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125036} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247377} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247379} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351453} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394414} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394421} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417184} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436248} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440470} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486397}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector in a New Basis Given Its Coordinates in the Standard Basis"} :step/id #uuid "9fa2d20c-055f-5364-a1f7-12e38362db0d" :step/math-academy-id 17542 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125667}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125498 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124577 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124384 :question/id #uuid "f04f55d3-40ec-52a8-a762-d8ac7090b8b8" :question/math-academy-id "e-7276"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "36f8f02f-acbd-5930-bd0c-8675ac868577" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048920}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124992} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125041} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247393} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247395} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351440} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351443} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351454} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386703} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394449} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417188} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417189} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417191} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436249} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440463} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440467} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440468}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector in a New Basis Given Its Coordinates in Another Basis"} :step/id #uuid "2c41659c-3fb0-58d9-904f-2296e677eb6c" :step/math-academy-id 26946} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125500 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125822 :tutorial/content "We've seen that to find the coordinates of a vector $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to a basis $\\mathcal{C}$ given its coordinates relative to a basis $\\mathcal B,$ we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [\\,x\\,]_{B}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn expanded form, the formula looks as follows:\n![](images/89/890f34433211786e2cb8aa71057de40bdf3b12678c2bbb5a79ef5e680555937f.svg)\n\nAlso, remember that the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}$ and from $\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}$ to $\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}$ are the inverses of each other:\n\n$$\nP_{\\small{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}\\to{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}}} = P_{\\small{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}}^{-1}\n\t\\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad\n\tP_{\\small{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}} = P_{\\small{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}\\to{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}}}^{-1}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "cfffdf8e-9e9f-5304-8249-54a094cc7c11" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4435 :tutorial/title "The Change-of-Basis Formula in Expanded Form"} :step/id #uuid "4c4259ae-ebd7-5d31-ae41-72156cefc066" :step/math-academy-id 27272 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125362}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125667 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125708 :tutorial/content "Let $\\mathcal{B}=\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2 \\}$ and $\\mathcal{C}=\\{ \\mathbf{c}_1,\\mathbf{c}_2 \\}$ be bases of $\\mathbb{R}^2.$ Now, suppose we would like to find the coordinates of $\\mathbf{b}_1$ and $\\mathbf{b}_2$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}.$ \n\n- We first need to find $x_1$ and $x_2$ such that \n\n$$\nx_1 \\mathbf{c}_1+x_2 \\mathbf{c}_2 = \\mathbf{b}_1,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to solving the system with the augmented matrix\n![](images/58/58010a2a5206e3fc27e3d924de70cf32dcc92c3578bdb2821ace7278fbb223cf.svg)\n\n- Similarly, we need to find $y_1$ and $y_2$ such that \n\n$$\ny_1 \\mathbf{c}_1+y_2 \\mathbf{c}_2 = \\mathbf{b}_2,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to solving the system with the augmented matrix\n![](images/68/68788aa3e388c5cba14662a947c1b7afaacb95f611c256d11a7a6d7614c352b9.svg)\n\nWe can solve both systems using Gaussian elimination. However, we can do that simultaneously by considering the matrix\n![](images/4b/4b00a4cbd7831f6da6acd06a087d5453bd455a89eb81f6b3036b1b572bb064d1.svg)\n\nReducing the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination, we get a matrix of the form\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & {x}_{1} & {y}_{1} \\\\ 0 & 1 & {x}_{2} & {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the required coefficients will be stored in the columns on the right-hand side." :tutorial/id #uuid "d42ee304-2e1f-5cc2-b334-65d083ea7323" :tutorial/math-academy-id 13287 :tutorial/title "Using Gaussian Elimination to Find the Coordinates of a Vector in a New Basis"} :step/id #uuid "184b8c31-de1a-5e34-ad87-2f626c830182" :step/math-academy-id 462199 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125498}}] :activity/title "Changing a Basis Using the Change-of-Coordinates Matrix" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124710}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436253} :activity/id #uuid "3b5b7055-d6b7-5899-a741-daa60aef491a" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048769 :topic/id #uuid "bd333eb5-72e6-5eaa-be12-c6bf36306b90" :topic/math-academy-id 1910} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436250 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386696 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ef569771-c4e7-5291-a02a-eccb98516f64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}16 \\\\ 42\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386698} {:answer/id #uuid "19f40148-c027-5e57-83ca-f9ce5e2e487f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 13\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386699} {:answer/id #uuid "f2dbac90-d79d-5e74-a3e8-3e28846fd99d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386700} {:answer/id #uuid "8aadec7c-66e6-5275-836d-0b2d825fbd7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}28 \\\\ 18\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386701} {:answer/id #uuid "54154bb7-e3f6-55f0-b690-aed92908791b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-16 \\\\ 26\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386702}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386701} :answer-field/id #uuid "30675a92-d5d0-5d0c-a77d-9c525c8b8db4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386697}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6ed39867-0f3e-5f3d-ab65-536828c9ba67" :question/math-academy-id "q-113573" :question/problem "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ to $C=\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\}$ is given below. Find $[x]_{B}$ given that $x=2{c}_{1}-4{c}_{2}.$\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 5 & -8\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{C\\to B}$ from $C$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{C\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 5 & -8\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\cdot (-8)-(-3)\\cdot 5}\\begin{bmatrix}-8 & 3 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 & -3 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that since $x=2{c}_{1}-4{c}_{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 & -3 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}28 \\\\ 18\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "4de2c56b-39c0-5e14-a7fb-e708db903e6f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436251}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436251 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436248 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "426822c2-147e-581d-8735-96a3bffe8321" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-12 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436236} {:answer/id #uuid "5c39bc95-ae02-5c17-9a57-15db094b94c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436238} {:answer/id #uuid "89f6cf40-819f-5c6b-aeaf-667b443a0b60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 11\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436240} {:answer/id #uuid "e55ca324-fff5-5f74-ad7a-3c6af6f7f1cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}36 \\\\ 13\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436244} {:answer/id #uuid "ef9b888d-cda8-5e1c-bef9-2a98c4f74288" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-22 \\\\ 27\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436245}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436238} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1014636-1510-50f6-b488-e3fe8d24c13d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436247}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0e4c9378-b054-5da6-b199-f46a138a46d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-113603" :question/problem "$$\nB=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\},\\,[x]_{S}=\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the standard basis $S$ are given above. Find $[x]_{B}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S}.\n$$\n\nFirst, recall that the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is the matrix whose columns are the vectors of $B$ relative to the standard basis $S.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{S\\to B}$ from $S$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to S}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\cdot 4-6\\cdot 3}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -6 \\\\ -3 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2} & \\frac{5}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2} & \\frac{5}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "4f40a8b0-4293-52f4-8c95-85c75998d506" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436252}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436252 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247379 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05662116-9ed5-51f3-a508-b1ed64f2e349" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244359} {:answer/id #uuid "33a9596f-e4e0-5e92-b079-84b8cbdda157" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ -11\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244776} {:answer/id #uuid "7294001d-9a70-5e64-8833-bfc2b3f3c0db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}25 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245337} {:answer/id #uuid "b6b79f00-6365-55a2-9673-e57f0f1960c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}16 \\\\ 21\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245947} {:answer/id #uuid "f5b08e24-4041-566d-9201-1f4cbd15de31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}19 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246483}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244359} :answer-field/id #uuid "12b31fac-75a2-50ec-9978-bbb512fe8ff1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246582}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "162a4f62-2177-5623-998d-5746dbddde07" :question/math-academy-id "q-113606" :question/problem "$$\nB=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}\\},\\,[x]_{S}=\\begin{bmatrix}18 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the standard basis $S$ are given above. Find $[x]_{B}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S}.\n$$\n\nFirst, recall that the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is the matrix whose columns are the vectors of $B$ relative to the standard basis $S.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -7 \\\\ -3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{S\\to B}$ from $S$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to S}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -7 \\\\ -3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\cdot 6-(-7)\\cdot (-3)}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 7 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{7}{9} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{5}{9}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{7}{9} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{5}{9}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}18 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "77dfbfe9-ef7f-5713-be40-7bce1749b9a7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436254}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436253 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323807 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e412cca-3de6-5980-bb99-cf3bccf43fec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321257} {:answer/id #uuid "137c55e3-1ce7-52fa-871f-4778b481186a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321301} {:answer/id #uuid "930eac4b-99c1-548d-911a-4f0dbf0771a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322310} {:answer/id #uuid "9dd98043-a25b-5027-a9f0-593fb49217d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322391} {:answer/id #uuid "d2cab1f4-06ce-5d69-9f7d-a3f736d395e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322794}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322391} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e47d3d8-0d42-5d02-8f6a-ceceea2fa24c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323291}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "76994fe0-8052-50ba-9dfa-9fe567d9b172" :question/math-academy-id "q-36485" :question/problem "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $C$ are given below. What is $[x]_{B}?$ \n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{bmatrix},\\,[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{C\\to B}$ from $C$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{C\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-2)\\cdot (-4)-3\\cdot 3}\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "a2ad54ea-289c-5268-9e48-3009985055d0" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436250}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436254 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436249 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ef4749a-0f85-5a2d-a5e3-df28976db7b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436237} {:answer/id #uuid "75556eec-854e-532d-9072-0581f7715993" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}11 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436239} {:answer/id #uuid "a95465bd-ca1b-539b-b564-dc5450e2c673" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436241} {:answer/id #uuid "d0811b3a-bd66-51ab-9df6-b54f8983cb5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}13 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436242} {:answer/id #uuid "d3c4b9b8-c843-5d6e-98b0-ea1b8a725222" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436243}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436237} :answer-field/id #uuid "54566980-7132-5fea-9868-d89904943d38" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436246}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "8d0458bf-0ded-5bc5-82a5-015d07a4533d" :question/math-academy-id "q-113615" :question/problem "$$\nB=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\},\\,C=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\},\\,[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $C$ are given above. Find $[x]_{B}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ and $\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B$ is a matrix whose columns are the vectors of $C$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ relative to the basis $B.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-3){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & -1 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -\\frac{1}{2} & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{C\\to B}$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{C\\to B}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2} & -4 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2} & -4 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "f5e3b29d-0add-527d-a99b-25156b6317ea"}] :activity/title "Changing a Basis Using the Change-of-Coordinates Matrix" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436235}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486512} :activity/id #uuid "123b57a8-59a0-5d9a-805f-fed4b8283678" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048769 :topic/id #uuid "bd333eb5-72e6-5eaa-be12-c6bf36306b90" :topic/math-academy-id 1910} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486482 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486397 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e5944a2-6f72-5f21-8b24-e64767793229" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486018} {:answer/id #uuid "9f66dc53-0b31-5237-969a-2397bba21b1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486050} {:answer/id #uuid "ae2979c6-49e8-5225-824c-1a6f38548151" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486078} {:answer/id #uuid "b1ffe2c5-8479-52b3-b64d-d795dd8a896a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486084} {:answer/id #uuid "c6cc0c71-6572-5be7-b79e-f4264330ac2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486124}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486018} :answer-field/id #uuid "d71b80ef-6d4c-597c-814a-17f8214907ee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486325}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "72bf6680-369b-5d8c-9e49-1546d6428898" :question/math-academy-id "q-113598" :question/problem "$$\nB=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\},\\,[x]_{S}=\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the standard basis $S$ are given above. Find $[x]_{B}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S}.\n$$\n\nFirst, recall that the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is the matrix whose columns are the vectors of $B$ relative to the standard basis $S.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{S\\to B}$ from $S$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to S}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\cdot 2-4\\cdot 1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{3}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{3}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "50c856b2-5de1-5fc6-b8b6-216f5affc71d"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486506 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351454 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "277d51c6-ba77-5bde-a9da-6f2d8333899a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348786} {:answer/id #uuid "36849b8b-2c62-5eb1-bb53-ebb31890e317" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348938} {:answer/id #uuid "526976b5-686a-54a2-a200-041c5d48d668" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349197} {:answer/id #uuid "8056b724-3039-56ac-ac48-651200dd450e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349623} {:answer/id #uuid "9abeb6bd-73c2-5d7d-9344-2f69c899f0c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349825}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348938} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8cbbee3-2e8a-56b1-9089-55eda08012bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350995}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "49aa77db-aeb3-5225-bb69-221318751e9a" :question/math-academy-id "q-82718" :question/problem "$$\nB=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\},\\,C=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\},\\,[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B$ are given above. Find $[x]_{C}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ and $\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose columns are the vectors of $B$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ relative to the basis $C.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 & -6 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}[\\,x\\,]_{B} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "b02f895b-b4b9-567f-99da-8866008266c1" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486517}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486510 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125036 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b3fc777-c87e-5494-9361-753fc99b8a92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417145} {:answer/id #uuid "af2e1656-5790-518b-ae95-17ddec42b6bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417157} {:answer/id #uuid "c820d187-6507-5a27-8119-ca85d381bd74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417165} {:answer/id #uuid "d1949552-5c2a-51ed-85be-630d9f8df6a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417166} {:answer/id #uuid "fc368894-a420-5e78-a8ce-445dfc038e7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417173}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417166} :answer-field/id #uuid "77968fba-758e-5c47-b717-abe8d60fbc59" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417179}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "618e1711-d591-5872-b815-9d14ad33b674" :question/math-academy-id "q-37160" :question/problem "$$\nB=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 16\\end{bmatrix}\\},\\,[x]_{S}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the standard basis $S$ are given above. Find $[x]_{B}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S}.\n$$\n\nFirst, recall that the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is the matrix whose columns are the vectors of $B$ relative to the standard basis $S.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 16\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{S\\to B}$ from $S$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to S}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 16\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 16-3\\cdot 5}\\begin{bmatrix}16 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}16 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}16 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "cea4b00d-cc11-568c-8bd2-36b2db4582ec" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486506}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486512 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486427 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1696c49c-b8ba-5c9f-a1e2-a234d7a12381" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485804} {:answer/id #uuid "33eeaaae-80c9-51a7-944e-f618a820b3bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485862} {:answer/id #uuid "35031deb-a4ba-5495-89d6-18fe7aca9f79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485864} {:answer/id #uuid "bb2528e3-8cd1-5c53-9f19-daeb351a37d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486101} {:answer/id #uuid "e673df76-6d49-52f2-85e7-cffc12218f67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486189}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486101} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0731c0a-4746-54da-b38a-46b9b2e964b1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486320}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ae7fdbe5-ac31-5cc5-8e79-33235643a822" :question/math-academy-id "q-36502" :question/problem "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ to $C=\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\}$ is given below. Find $[x]_{B}$ given that $x={c}_{1}+4{c}_{2}.$\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 10\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{C\\to B}$ from $C$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{C\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 10\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 10-6\\cdot 3}\\begin{bmatrix}10 & -3 \\\\ -6 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that since $x={c}_{1}+4{c}_{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "d7f877e0-9517-58da-83be-55a89211a009" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486510}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486517 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486444 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15690e8b-c556-578a-a848-9e2012319066" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485801} {:answer/id #uuid "2ce04a03-a227-57a9-bb1f-9d7b6996fb5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485848} {:answer/id #uuid "972d0aed-9872-5a09-9680-0c04b590dfee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486037} {:answer/id #uuid "b85661ff-f141-5e76-a9a5-e29f75d6b6b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486097} {:answer/id #uuid "d2bcf6bc-21ea-5ab1-b19b-a0bda85a2f8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486151}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486097} :answer-field/id #uuid "f917e741-700b-51ed-9e8c-db10a05c79b2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486345}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "cd3d1f8f-1ec9-5277-bd94-fc774bb919de" :question/math-academy-id "q-113595" :question/problem "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ to $C=\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\}$ is given below. Find $[x]_{B}$ given that $x=2{c}_{1}-{c}_{2}.$\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{C\\to B}$ from $C$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{C\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\cdot 5-5\\cdot 3}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{4}{5}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that since $x=2{c}_{1}-{c}_{2},$ we obtain \n\n$$\n[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{4}{5}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "e7ff6d11-7ab8-5df7-ab96-cc2f9b5a933c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486482}}] :activity/title "Changing a Basis Using the Change-of-Coordinates Matrix" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485749}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 480.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125604} :activity/id #uuid "c46f3c61-cb8f-5de3-a323-513dfb71dceb" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048942 :topic/id #uuid "cdb710e4-10cf-5554-8041-f24c448bbdae" :topic/math-academy-id 1930} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125604 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125863 :tutorial/content "A **neighborhood** of a point $x_0\\in \\mathbb R$ is the set of points that are \"close to\" $x_0.$ \n\nMore precisely, let's define a number $\\delta >0$, where $\\delta$ is usually very small.  A **neighborhood** of a point $x_0$ is the set of all points whose distance from $x_0$ is smaller than $\\delta.$ \n\nIn one-dimensional space, a neighborhood of a point $x_0\\in\\mathbb R$ is an open interval $\\left(x_0-\\delta, x_0+\\delta\\right),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/04/04016c37be4c907eabc144db16eb757dff773907262ccd04941cf27ddd2e49d6.png)\n\nFor example, if we set $\\delta = 0.1,$ then the corresponding neighborhood of the point $x_0 = 2$ is as follows:\n\n![](images/03/0329bef56b8b903eb13510b7344fd7ca488d34c8d0c85c7648cebc8ecf7cfa40.png)\n\nNote that $x=1.9$ and $x=2.1$ are *not* included in this neighborhood since their distance from the point $x_0$ is *not* smaller than $\\delta.$ \n\nWe can represent a neighborhood of $x_0$ using set notation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{x : | x- x_0| < \\delta \\}\n$$\n\nWe often refer to sets of this type as *open* neighborhoods since they contain all points whose distance from $x_0$ is *smaller than* (but not equal) to $\\delta.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "b2ea7b59-5287-5d12-b53f-32a9254b8ba7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6818 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "44c26f5a-57b1-5e3b-b126-e2f92e8f7bac" :step/math-academy-id 41386 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125622}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125605 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124634 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124191 :question/id #uuid "da9684b4-df8d-531d-a200-cf54685067c8" :question/math-academy-id "e-10913"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "69b456ea-44de-59f0-b2c8-bc5b9495974f" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124984} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125215} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241034} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278226} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278227} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278228} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376117} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377648} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377655} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417815} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417818}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements From a Diagram"} :step/id #uuid "13578b7b-3533-5f2d-b6bc-c8214f0f163b" :step/math-academy-id 41387 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125615}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125606 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124635 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124192 :question/id #uuid "7e78e3a1-836a-53af-95a3-0a9d192cc7d8" :question/math-academy-id "e-10924"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b34c9c06-e884-5758-a184-39330a07d8cd" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050229}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124795} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124809} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278219} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278220} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278224} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278225} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376096} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376103} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376110} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435511}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Interior and Boundary Points of Sets of Real Numbers"} :step/id #uuid "b2201caf-3e1b-53b4-b391-8a27cfbbdce1" :step/math-academy-id 41433 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125607}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125607 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125864 :tutorial/content "In two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of a point $\\mathbf x_0\\in \\mathbb R^2$ is a disc of radius $\\delta$ centered at $\\mathbf x_0$ that does not include the bounding circle, as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nThe definitions of the interior and boundary points are the same as for the one-dimensional case. The only difference is that now a neighborhood of a point is a disc.\n\nFor example, consider the set $S$ and points $A,$ $B, C,$ and $D,$ shown below.\n\n![](images/a7/a7e037fd998c235ff7b721e7187d44814700ac5f41a256920f34f248c798c520.png)\n\nLet's classify each of these as interior or boundary points (or neither):\n\n- $A$ is an interior point since *there exists* a neighborhood of $A$ that is fully contained inside the set.\n\n- $B$ is neither an interior nor a boundary point because *there exists* a neighborhood of $B$ that does not contain points from the set.\n\n- $C$ is a boundary point since *every* neighborhood of $C$ contains points that lie in the set and points that do not lie in the set.\n\n- $D$ is a boundary point since *every* neighborhood of $D$ contains points that lie in the set and points that do not lie in the set.\n\nNotice that although $C$ and $D$ are both boundary points, $C\\notin S$ and yet $D\\in S.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "bfda97a2-2fe7-5c9a-a067-dbf903ba2111" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6825 :tutorial/title "Interior and Boundary Points of Sets in the Plane"} :step/id #uuid "fd03cb93-6f1c-502a-9888-3a2700a9c8a7" :step/math-academy-id 41436 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125605}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125615 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124641 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124199 :question/id #uuid "1f21955b-70dc-5b61-8b19-42860de1be4e" :question/math-academy-id "e-11031"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "64ba57cc-a3eb-582a-963b-b94895a20bac" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050095} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050153} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050367}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124759} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124982} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278201} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278202} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278203} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278208} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278218} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376082} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376089} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417816} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417817}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Interior and Boundary Points From Linear Inequalities"} :step/id #uuid "23fe8ae7-b1b8-5490-93e8-49b20cd742f0" :step/math-academy-id 41912 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125616}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125616 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124642 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124200 :question/id #uuid "9b01032c-d4e6-5660-a3c9-477e67c011a7" :question/math-academy-id "e-11032"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "24b9af85-3717-59e0-aa72-d701f3c6b6a8" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048795} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049570} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049699}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124805} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124983} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125216} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241040} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241042} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241043} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278221} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278222} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375638} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375645} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478455}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Interior and Boundary Points From Nonlinear Inequalities"} :step/id #uuid "dc8051c9-6e2f-5ded-82a6-e902adddd2c5" :step/math-academy-id 41916} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125622 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125870 :tutorial/content "Now, suppose that $S$ is a set of real numbers. A point $x_0$ is called an **interior point** of $S$ if *some* neighborhood of $\\mathbf x_0$ is *fully contained* inside $S.$ \n\nFor example, consider the set $S=[0,1)$ and the points $A$ and $B,$ shown below.\n\n![](images/a1/a12c60b6f60ca7c35162fb1ddf0d0d507fe80e73e621c451c21f0b882034ae21.png)\n\nThe point $A$ is an interior point of $S,$ but $B$ is not:\n\n- The point $A$ is an interior point of $S$ since there exists a neighborhood of $A$ that is *fully contained* inside $S.$\n\n- The point $B$ is *not* an interior point of $S$ since there is no neighborhood of $B$ that is *fully contained* inside $S.$ In other words, any neighborhood of $B$ contains at least one point *outside* the set.\n\nNote that neither $x=0$ nor $x=1$ are interior points. To see why, let's draw a neighborhood of each point:\n\n![](images/a9/a923732241443a0fd6a5f53dab7de2d346f5704a0b034c276e64dc3df2746f4f.png)\n\n \n\nFrom here, we can deduce the following:\n\n- The point $x=0$ is *not* an interior point of $S$ since there is no neighborhood of $x=0$ that is *fully* contained inside $S.$\n\n- The point $x=1$ is also *not* an interior point of $S$ since there is no neighborhood of $x=1$ that is *fully* contained inside $S.$\n\nThe set containing all interior points of a set $S$ is called the **interior** of $S,$ and is denoted $\\textrm{int}\\,S.$ \n\nFor the set $[0,1),$ its interior is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{int}\\,S = (0,1).\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "da6d3c21-fe0a-566e-a6e0-117f10988923" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7685 :tutorial/title "Interior Points"} :step/id #uuid "99fecfa4-9502-52e4-aff9-d84b074446bb" :step/math-academy-id 44624 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125623}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125623 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125871 :tutorial/content "A point $x_0$ is a **boundary point** of $S$ if *every* neighborhood of $x_0$ contains points that *are* in $S$ *and* points that are *not* in $S.$ \n\nLet's consider the set $S=[0,1),$ the points $x=0, x=1,$ and neighborhoods of these points.\n\n![](images/a9/a923732241443a0fd6a5f53dab7de2d346f5704a0b034c276e64dc3df2746f4f.png)\n\nBoth $x=0$ and $x=1$ are boundary points of $S{:}$\n\n- The point $x=0$ is a boundary point because *every* open neighborhood contains points that belong to $S$ and points that do not belong to $S.$\n\n- The point $x=1$ is a boundary point because *every* open neighborhood contains points that belong to $S$ and points that do not belong to $S.$\n\nThe set of all boundary points of a set $S$ is called the **boundary** of $S,$ and is denoted as $\\partial S.$\n\nFor the set $[0,1)$ above, its boundary contains two points only, namely $x=0$ and $x=1,$ and we write\n\n$$\n\\partial S = \\{0,1\\}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "54b0ce40-c312-54e6-87c5-ba2b69af3dae" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7686 :tutorial/title "Boundary Points"} :step/id #uuid "2b346783-d47e-5992-9033-dcf4627c7e39" :step/math-academy-id 44625 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125606}}] :activity/title "Interior and Boundary Points" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124723}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417821} :activity/id #uuid "1525b4dc-2341-50b4-9999-2ad38292c9ae" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048942 :topic/id #uuid "cdb710e4-10cf-5554-8041-f24c448bbdae" :topic/math-academy-id 1930} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417819 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417815 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f955c119-f900-5747-9855-7eb87295f2f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473260} {:answer/id #uuid "dcc29abc-f9e8-53f8-a218-d470e670dac2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473261}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186473261} :answer-field/id #uuid "c0b57ac4-5833-5f98-85f6-8d01d8cfbdee" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474025} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "da87805c-dd59-5cd4-a00e-ea9010aca456" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473262} {:answer/id #uuid "ce41ce68-62e4-5bd8-8ba7-9e969d0aef89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473263}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186473262} :answer-field/id #uuid "99f96f24-dff3-5ad2-ae8b-117ac4e4cad2" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474026} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8eb19004-1309-5d14-8763-0cdb11e1bbb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473264} {:answer/id #uuid "64416903-13ab-5932-b3c4-6697b70d4774" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473265}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186473264} :answer-field/id #uuid "85b6a004-2b46-516a-adc3-ac69774aa23d" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474027}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4db015a0-682b-5ff4-909b-ad19f05ab168" :question/math-academy-id "q-71826" :question/problem "![](images/b8/b838b945d0b4002d8ae2238af5992c83dcf23b2985c448c716a5caf8de31b369.png)\n\nConsider the set $M,$ depicted by the shaded region above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ is a boundary point of the set $M$\n- $B$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n- $C$ is a boundary point of the set $M$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n![](images/be/be1ece00243777f3f6b6973319e15ea1e5c8556b2452b61a451713f9dcb86595.png)\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $A$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $A$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\n- Statement II is false. The point $B$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $B$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\n- Statement III is false. The point $C$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $C$ that is fully contained inside the set $M.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I only'."} :step/id #uuid "05f3aec1-db2e-5c3d-b48a-0200d46f45e8" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417822}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417820 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417818 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24c50983-8391-5870-8348-d7f660e46076" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472748} {:answer/id #uuid "60e11f58-a5dd-5c6e-b89c-b922fde1c5fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472749}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186472749} :answer-field/id #uuid "143a9e68-361d-54b4-9f3d-e48cc583f56a" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473769} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6913eb54-9ddc-554e-82da-aa99b31eea06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472750} {:answer/id #uuid "03c27322-4cea-5897-a805-a6c4a07342da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472751}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186472751} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c72ab19-8f7b-5385-a77a-7bded7712b10" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473770} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e9f94601-7133-564b-ac38-53504ef01422" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472752} {:answer/id #uuid "b58541fc-27f2-5130-a4c2-74d24b8dffa6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472753}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186472753} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d9085bd-5d71-5a86-abfb-326a5e169143" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473771}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f7105850-e717-5535-8a28-db3aaf9f3031" :question/math-academy-id "q-159495" :question/problem "![](images/19/19174ec33e43c900773031043d5de200e892f5e3ee51704d0800b6a944147155.png)\n\nConsider the set $M,$ depicted by the shaded region above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n- $B$ is a boundary point of the set $M$\n- $C$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n![](images/43/43362800e91cbb57c22fc6851c10f00927a030a790dea9284e69a5c7c3d4ae25.png)\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $A$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $A$ that is fully contained inside the set $M.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, $B$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $B$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $C$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $C$ that is fully contained inside the set $M.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I, II, and III'."} :step/id #uuid "09199b9d-3f40-5adc-9fbb-571a33f5f584"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417821 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124983 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23591f63-c86d-5f91-9a17-a690666923d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448578} {:answer/id #uuid "be31c6af-3a33-55f9-8fe1-63efb93650a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448579}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186448579} :answer-field/id #uuid "26a890ed-051b-51e6-b54d-7c194bdaf7f9" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449329} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e844f5b-4f06-5bf8-946e-b743a531b0af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448580} {:answer/id #uuid "1847ce53-72b0-5d27-b50b-d4102ef44f22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448581}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186448581} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c644840-d5ba-52dd-ae49-d42747e8e8ca" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449330} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "68a62ca0-3bd2-53c6-b360-1371ecc0ba04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448582} {:answer/id #uuid "587abaee-0245-5ac8-bcc3-421b0ac4056a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448583}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186448583} :answer-field/id #uuid "78ebfedd-9fc3-5873-908e-1133f982c35c" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449331}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5b10043b-af03-5e4b-8ef7-b7ee2892ed6d" :question/math-academy-id "q-36409" :question/problem "The set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are given by\n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4},\\,∂A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The point $P(\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})$ lies on the boundary of $A$\n- **II.** The point $P(\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})$ belongs to $A$\n- **III.** The point $Q(-1,0)$ is an interior point of $A$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$ \n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $A$ consists of all points that lie on or inside the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](images/bb/bbc3e1bfb40c12cfdc655b4207c5f34ea0a01ce254b3f04b96b05ffbbad3f5af.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})$ and $Q(-1,0)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](images/10/100a2b2c1aa58cdfa59980cae4253e1de8b4b2c984c0793c66f7e7cc6edb5211.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of $P(\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})$ satisfy the equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $∂A,$ the boundary of $A.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The coordinates of $P(\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})$ satisfy the inequality ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $A.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $Q$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $Q$ that is fully contained inside the set $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I, II, and III.'"} :step/id #uuid "15c853c6-73a1-5b09-aa32-230241fa286d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417819}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417822 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417817 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ca900fdb-2d62-5862-8f58-5e2c93efbb46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453436} {:answer/id #uuid "839c8d82-74a4-5464-b707-309afd311002" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453437}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453437} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e5b6f56-ac9e-5312-8788-c82ee0344df5" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454468} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9eb2be68-e58e-5cf5-a802-398a76d09aee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453438} {:answer/id #uuid "ae1f7b94-44bb-5eab-8fad-f6e5da807f42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453439}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453439} :answer-field/id #uuid "49d1f656-b70c-5cbc-b720-c740ebcddde4" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454469} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "66605df2-8aaf-504d-949d-2dc2c5eb81cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453440} {:answer/id #uuid "fbb9751c-c1ad-5871-aa80-62619467576e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453441}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453441} :answer-field/id #uuid "54080e51-b892-57e7-b433-ee3c85f02bbe" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454470}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "dbe53a96-4c4c-5897-ba48-d4861ec6cb49" :question/math-academy-id "q-158547" :question/problem "The set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are given by\n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:y>2x-3},\\,∂A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:y=2x-3}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The point $P(1,-1)$ lies on the boundary of $A$\n- **II.** The point $Q(0,0)$ belongs to $A$\n- **III.** The point $Q(0,0)$ is an interior point of $A$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $A$ consists of all points that lie above the line $y=2x-3.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](images/5a/5af40a400e5ea4942a1cf89b09184a277df96d252e4a7fee41bd2df7024a631f.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(1,-1)$ and $Q(0,0)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](images/27/27b978864eac65c93c7886b2d54dfede7130f0f93a16ef96421cd0e5741a6940.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of $P(1,-1)$ satisfy $y=2x-3.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $∂A,$ the boundary of $A.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the coordinates of $Q(0,0)$ satisfy the inequality $y>2x-3.$ Hence, $Q$ lies in the set $A.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, there exists an open neighborhood of $Q$ that is entirely contained inside the set $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I, II, and III.'"} :step/id #uuid "209dd426-c134-5aa2-9527-59de41410725" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417823}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417823 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417816 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6fb12b54-0323-5408-9f63-bd359e7b2e14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453448} {:answer/id #uuid "164d2254-207a-594f-a70d-382d51b3f8e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453449}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453449} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5032cdf-c59e-5e33-880d-955f6d02841e" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454474} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3427dac4-97f1-5765-aafc-94068cf9677e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453450} {:answer/id #uuid "6c0ad7a7-e7bc-5678-aca2-5d4474e68307" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453451}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453450} :answer-field/id #uuid "e3387072-c1bc-59ee-8d45-dc6cd0e5411d" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454475} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42ad1ee2-a34e-5097-bfd1-c65783c31fe4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453452} {:answer/id #uuid "0e0e000c-633e-51cc-bcaf-35de257a2bd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453453}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453452} :answer-field/id #uuid "feaa10ce-c2f4-5d9a-998c-4aa5896d0d74" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454476}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7e4c0564-b5b1-55bc-bc17-3dffa3a951a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-158601" :question/problem "The set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are given by\n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:y<-1},\\,∂A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:y=-1}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The point $P(1,-1)$ lies on the boundary of $A$\n- **II.** The point $P(1,-1)$ belongs to $A$\n- **III.** The point $Q(2,0)$ is an interior point of $A$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $A$ consists of all points that lie below the line $y=-1.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](images/75/75d5d199d12cc2f3170822a40c52762fcc8368caac26a8134661f9ac72ab462b.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(1,-1)$ and $Q(2,0)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](images/81/818da293be040897d3a536b396d6fe7ff992f3d91f976301e14a6253356b5bde.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of $P(1,-1)$ satisfy $y=-1.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $∂A,$ the boundary of $A.$\n\n- Statement II is false. The coordinates of $P(-1,1)$ do not satisfy the inequality $y<-1.$ Hence, $P$ does not lie in the set $A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, $Q$ is not an interior point of $A$ since any neighborhood of $Q$ contains at least one point *outside* the set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I only.'"} :step/id #uuid "b1e2a497-e042-518c-bac4-15b77f002a69" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417820}}] :activity/title "Interior and Boundary Points" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417784}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481682} :activity/id #uuid "2e2eeeff-af14-5c58-b935-c783ef93c698" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048942 :topic/id #uuid "cdb710e4-10cf-5554-8041-f24c448bbdae" :topic/math-academy-id 1930} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481682 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478455 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "435fbf66-881a-599f-a5de-59147174d9f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477860} {:answer/id #uuid "469b4926-a18a-5a39-87cf-c8473bed4b47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477872} {:answer/id #uuid "89babb92-9de9-5f2f-b11d-064346a1f1ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478013} {:answer/id #uuid "99e2c243-585a-5ab6-a643-ace8c4066e27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478033} {:answer/id #uuid "abf4ed0d-fc7b-5e79-816f-b20987508d59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478064}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186477872} :answer-field/id #uuid "1eb96924-1d1d-506d-a661-5befdb8dbe4a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478262}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e5330869-80f9-574d-be15-bcaa4f259731" :question/math-academy-id "q-36429" :question/problem "The set $B$ and its boundary $∂B$ are given by\n\n$$\nB=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}<1\\},\\,∂B=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}=1\\}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The point $P(\\sqrt{2},1)$ lies on the boundary of $B$\n\nII. The point $P(\\sqrt{2},1)$ belongs to $B$\n\nIII. The point $Q(0,-1)$ is an interior point of $B$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n\\{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta \\}\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $B$ consists of all points that lie inside the ellipse $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}=1.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](images/97/97ded2e99adb83b69bc547cef822c46ac2134d3ed17c9cdf65f24083560afae5.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(\\sqrt{2},1)$ and $Q(0,-1)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](images/38/38a11d6712843367b08ff98843b3c3daf43729d82adbe70e15b8019fb68cee6c.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of $P(\\sqrt{2},1)$ satisfy the equation $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}=1.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $∂B,$ the boundary of $B.$\n\n- Statement II is false. The coordinates of $P(\\sqrt{2},1)$ do not satisfy the inequality $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}<1.$ Hence, $P$ does not belongs to $B.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $Q$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $Q$ that is fully contained inside the set $B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I and III only'."} :step/id #uuid "1c6c6de6-cfd8-5a68-9102-8e31ed8dc6af" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481723}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481687 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376082 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f9d5b57d-d397-5e8b-9a46-ca703dc128d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453430} {:answer/id #uuid "654fdd13-26b0-5b13-a201-e47a6bbd127f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453431}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453431} :answer-field/id #uuid "f245c56b-ea24-5bc6-9714-193572353072" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454465} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c17641c7-2616-5f44-913a-204e61d02795" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453432} {:answer/id #uuid "1798276f-6c7e-5444-91e4-9f13f58ce650" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453433}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453433} :answer-field/id #uuid "2595354c-4c90-5eb8-bc56-8aa27847f7cd" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454466} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7f28fa17-78af-5e9c-9940-0619d6581c75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453434} {:answer/id #uuid "03c6eb37-ee8b-5456-b1b0-d792a68992e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453435}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453434} :answer-field/id #uuid "55044069-05bd-53df-bf38-5763c4c860a7" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454467}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "80400990-b978-5e6c-95c6-54cde07862e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-158534" :question/problem "The set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are given by\n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:y\\le x+2},\\,∂A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:y=x+2}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The point $P(2,4)$ lies on the boundary of $A$\n- **II.** The point $P(2,4)$ belongs to $A$\n- **III.** The point $Q(-2,3)$ is an interior point of $A$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $A$ consists of all points that lie on or below the line $y=x+2.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](images/3b/3b0505f0c1fb295665dd64c9d0f26f23960b638ef0e1d414c7ad306a89154dfb.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(2,4)$ and $Q(-2,3)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](images/62/623f00c423c7a846c52537d58c071c730083ba963fb488dbedbb76637ea9cc8c.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of $P(2,4)$ satisfy the equation $y=x+2.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $∂A,$ the boundary of $A.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The coordinates of $P(2,4)$ satisfy the inequality $y\\le x+4.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, $Q$ is not an interior point of $A$ since any neighborhood of $Q$ contains at least one point *outside* the set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I and II only.'"} :step/id #uuid "310f06ad-505e-54b4-996b-7dc30a68d2ee" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481705}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481701 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278228 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d436c48-b5c4-585f-a38c-5dc69c2efe0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464636} {:answer/id #uuid "c71daeb2-9b0f-5776-bfeb-ca2ae15dac67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464637}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186464637} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8cc30b0-4919-5870-8b93-d9d25272b3a6" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465252} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b87f9f55-43d7-553b-9dfe-3a193c6e8fe0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464638} {:answer/id #uuid "def2f8dd-7369-5c86-a313-88ab5e541789" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464639}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186464639} :answer-field/id #uuid "34182fca-1665-5d7a-8612-d5439b2e05ab" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465253} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ee0abd33-f10b-55b9-a007-2ea3f5540421" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464640} {:answer/id #uuid "1e6f1c65-b9cc-55d0-abbc-f7595924d4a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464641}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186464641} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d9d88a2-2fcb-55a4-b7cf-4375c94b9208" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465254}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9430b4bf-f1ad-5467-9486-5b90e2e64bb6" :question/math-academy-id "q-159550" :question/problem "![](images/0e/0e999290971eed02d7f12f9154bdb4d04f3328fd250b385059d94e83e3f195b2.png)\n\nConsider the set $M,$ depicted by the shaded region above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ is a boundary point of the set $M$\n- $B$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n- $C$ is a boundary point of the set $M$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n![](images/f1/f10ff87f64dc07adb8e9535926186341bab88f2d0c783475ef8fd57b06b35fc0.png)\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $A$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $A$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, $B$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $B$ that is fully contained inside the set $M.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $C$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $C$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I, II, and III'."} :step/id #uuid "5defe44d-af9c-50de-9c3a-215ee05792f2"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481705 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376103 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9eb02c31-7aaa-5f27-b88d-0bac5e5ad915" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471095} {:answer/id #uuid "b97e2bd8-7962-5731-ba0e-3fbbb462b382" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471096}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186471096} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d50bd4b-9d59-580e-80c2-44d2596ae2d2" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472053} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2061a619-a5e4-54b1-91b8-46d58be4ebda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471097} {:answer/id #uuid "0fe9756c-7be4-5f9a-b46e-b99e86db76bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471098}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186471097} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a8ac79c-036b-59a8-86e0-7503a1be86e8" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472054} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c8bab23-b03e-52ee-ae9d-30f1b5066d53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471099} {:answer/id #uuid "5d0e0cf0-0b66-548e-8160-cac53743805a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471100}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186471100} :answer-field/id #uuid "9cffa612-f7f9-54e4-b510-6f7c9d3f046b" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472055}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "82499ed8-8929-5ffd-bdf0-732a59ce454e" :question/math-academy-id "q-159265" :question/problem "![](images/c4/c4a50ae5f62dc5c52d0060a969d510453d3f922fad8f7ba887b2b1ee77fceae6.png)\n\nConsider the set $M=(-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty )$ and the points $A,$ $B,$ and $C,$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ is a boundary point of the set $M$\n- $B$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n- $C$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}\\in R$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is *smaller than* $\\delta :$ \n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nA neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is given by the open interval $({x}_{0}-\\delta ,{x}_{0}+\\delta ),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/f8/f889352f25e7936d0e5ea76585a979efbb301da61f7e99a937e979af93ee0664.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The point $A$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $A$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\n![](images/e8/e847aa005e917a88b19566933e3770edd05ddee946fc25dfd6fed91016bb909d.png)\n\n- Statement II is false. The point $B$ is *not* an interior point of $M$ since any neighborhood of $B$ contains at least one point *outside* the set.\n\n![](images/51/51439f6021efd768268e59c63627449cc68c9a420c4c7a80ab6773dd46e6ec1f.png)\n\n- Statement III is true. The point $C$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $C$ that is fully contained inside the set $M.$\n\n![](images/dd/ddd471167f604b4ba97b36ddff3af6be7c7d3cfad6021a4cfc779c6f8ebb2a3d.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I and III only.'"} :step/id #uuid "64801b9b-8548-5aee-9429-d836c284e4aa" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481701}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481723 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124984 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06ad12bb-2aee-5f41-ab40-c7e07a29ecc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459865} {:answer/id #uuid "01c3dbc7-f7af-57f0-b1fa-a62993667554" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459866}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459866} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8d9e536-a559-5926-85d7-959a922313a8" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460799} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e2326431-abad-5546-8bd6-d752a2d42a78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459867} {:answer/id #uuid "95aa5abb-9709-5cff-931e-02fc205a698b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459868}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459868} :answer-field/id #uuid "7570d9d3-0c06-5e02-b6ac-3f8b39e3de82" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460800} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44150225-85ba-53e8-894f-8dc3b204e3e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459869} {:answer/id #uuid "c060cc95-7b2b-536a-80dc-89828039a2ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459870}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459869} :answer-field/id #uuid "bac9c0b8-6db5-5d97-8aab-3bb2c71951f1" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460801}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "560c5161-e608-551f-9c8f-70f39e328770" :question/math-academy-id "q-36432" :question/problem "![](images/a5/a50bf96047d64846b704a54ce965c9cf2c8c7916810b26849aacffe3ace84e60.png)\n\nConsider the set $M,$ depicted by the shaded region above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n- $B$ is a boundary point of the set $M$\n- $C$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$ \n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n![](images/90/9072fe06b5821810a152f992cdd3d852ccb4cd0744c3a63119ba2634d3e3121d.png)\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $A$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $A$ that is fully contained inside the set $M.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The point $B$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $B$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Every open neighborhood of $C$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$ The point $C$ is a boundary point for the given set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I and II only'."} :step/id #uuid "9b477457-6b55-5d87-a037-106ef4b7ead7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481687}}] :activity/title "Interior and Boundary Points" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481566}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134901} :activity/id #uuid "fc007430-4cc9-562b-9192-4d3c9dccd500" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049312 :topic/id #uuid "eb826c2f-ae04-5de5-b6a6-7efcf7e974ae" :topic/math-academy-id 2201} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134901 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135151 :tutorial/content "If an $n\\!\\times \\!n$ matrix $A$ has an entire row or column of zeros, then its determinant is equal to zero.\n\nFor example, consider the matrix $A$ given below:\n\n![](images/6b/6bb8b64f7ed8730e641cdf88fc6fe7add40bc15061095c6b1f57c1b764b28feb.svg)\n\nThe second row consists of all zeros, and the asterisks ($\\ast$) may represent any number.\n\nLet's compute the determinant using the Laplace expansion across the second row:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}∗ & ∗ & ∗ \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ ∗ & ∗ & ∗\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-1)^{2+1}(0)|\\begin{aligned}∗ & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗\\end{aligned}|+(-1)^{2+2}(0)|\\begin{aligned}∗ & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗\\end{aligned}|+(-1)^{2+3}(0)|\\begin{aligned}∗ & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =0+0+0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that it doesn't matter what numbers are represented by the asterisks ($\\ast$). Each cofactor is multiplied by zero, so the final result must be zero." :tutorial/id #uuid "cee0a552-60f2-5785-a506-63bd053364c7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6888 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "b68428f5-f5bb-5090-b7f9-4971dc931914" :step/math-academy-id 41745 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134902}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134902 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135152 :tutorial/content "Now, suppose a matrix has no zero rows but it has two identical rows:\n\n![](images/74/7477781b618ec384b1638d01707acf545e7d9882ffca5bbc0efde4e224b04308.svg)\n\nWe can subtract the $1$st row from the $3$rd row, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this operation does not change the determinant, we get $\\det(A) = \\det(B).$ Furthermore, as $B$ contains a zero row, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\det(A) = \\det(B) = 0.\n$$\n\nNotice that we can use this method with columns too. Therefore, we have the following property:\n\n*If a square matrix has at least two identical rows/columns, its determinant equals zero.*" :tutorial/id #uuid "6813d49e-3908-5735-8196-8e1aad5a14e6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6889 :tutorial/title "Determinants with Identical Rows or Identical Columns"} :step/id #uuid "e105e6ae-64b8-5812-85e8-b5c3ef7b6c7d" :step/math-academy-id 41746 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134903}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134903 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133911 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133466 :question/id #uuid "bb2795c3-6359-50d8-9531-1e9dfae7a2ce" :question/math-academy-id "e-10994"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "28142e26-f6c7-54c9-9c16-035c75f7659f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048462} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049159}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134395} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134408} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272009} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329700} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351408} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400867} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420351} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420352} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427636} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484213} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484278}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Determinant of a Matrix With A Zero Row/Column or Two Identical Rows/Columns"} :step/id #uuid "f2a27f52-c605-5d01-84c1-49531f59d922" :step/math-academy-id 41747 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134904}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134904 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135153 :tutorial/content "Now, suppose a matrix has no zero rows and no identical rows but it has two proportional rows:\n\n![](images/81/8181d36ff1378310744b0d74f42f2934eb1e41053f45ecfeacf0c813abe994df.svg)\n\nWe can add three times the $1$st row to the $3$rd row, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -6 & -3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this operation does not change the determinant, we get $\\det(A) = \\det(B).$ Furthermore, as $B$ contains a zero row, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\det(A) = \\det(B) = 0.\n$$\n\nNotice that we can use this method with columns too. Therefore, we have the following property:\n\n*If a square matrix has at least two proportional rows/columns, its determinant equals zero.*" :tutorial/id #uuid "fe83e8c9-948a-596f-a2d9-c2ae2fe4f335" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6890 :tutorial/title "Determinants with Proportional Rows/Columns"} :step/id #uuid "23e26677-bdeb-5a45-a6d3-4a8d02a72732" :step/math-academy-id 41750 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134905}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134905 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133912 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133467 :question/id #uuid "dc780837-b763-5ba9-981a-2373afcb524a" :question/math-academy-id "e-10995"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8aef330a-81b3-56c4-9559-67df673e8687" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134394} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134407} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272011} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329654} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351409} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402861} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402868} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411852} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415475} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415477} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415478} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484193}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Determinant of a Matrix With Two Proportional Rows/Columns"} :step/id #uuid "93111f5e-5fd2-5a5b-81b1-27969a9b5d1d" :step/math-academy-id 41751 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134906}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134906 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135154 :tutorial/content "In conclusion, if an $n\\!\\times \\!n$ matrix $A$ satisfies any one of the following conditions, then $\\det(A)=0.$\n\nI. $A$ has a zero row/column\n\nII. $A$ has two identical rows/columns\n\nIII. $A$ has two proportional rows/columns\n\nIV. $A$ has a row/column that is a linear combination of other rows/columns\n\nNotice that condition IV encompasses each of the conditions I, II, and III. If we have a zero row/column, two identical rows/columns, or proportional rows/columns, then the rows/columns form a linearly dependent set of vectors. \nIn turn, this means that one of the rows/columns is a linear combination of other rows/columns.\n\n**Watch out!** While all of the above conditions are *sufficient* to conclude that $\\det(A)=0,$ only condition IV is *necessary* to conclude that $\\det(A)=0.$ \nIn other words, if we have a matrix $A$ with $\\det(A)=0,$ then conditions I, II, and III may or may not be satisfied, while condition IV *must* be satisfied. \n\nSo, we have the following theorem:\n\n*$\\det(A)=0$ if and only if one of the rows/columns of $A$ is a linear combination of other rows/columns of $A.$*\n\nFinally, note also that in the case of a $2\\times 2$ matrix *only*, statements III and IV mean the same thing." :tutorial/id #uuid "9d8b0323-170c-5a60-b372-1a258c4848cc" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6891 :tutorial/title "Conditions When a Determinant Equals Zero"} :step/id #uuid "4aec093b-050b-5263-9ef2-c875455b8c07" :step/math-academy-id 41754 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134908}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134907 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133913 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133468 :question/id #uuid "33ca37e5-c598-52a2-861f-2d5bfa6fd571" :question/math-academy-id "e-10996"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "abaeea03-6a2a-5d37-a2af-a9ea109175ad" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134393} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134414} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272012} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272013} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329652} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329653} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329699} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351410} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415476} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415479} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420353}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Reasons Why a Determinant Equals Zero"} :step/id #uuid "81571aa7-ce3d-5713-bf67-a49538fee0ef" :step/math-academy-id 41755} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134908 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135155 :tutorial/content "Geometrically, the absolute value of an $n \\times n$ determinant can be interpreted as the volume of the $n$-dimensional parallelepiped spanned by the columns of the determinant. \n\nFor example:\n\n- Suppose the columns of a $3\\times 3$ matrix $A$ are ${\\color{black}\\mathbf{a}}, {\\color{black}\\mathbf{b}},$ and ${\\color{black}\\mathbf{c}}.$\n![](images/6e/6e046341b7a1b8f6760797f7db89a7e021a6ace6b5cdf28bd5217998cb450837.svg)\nThe $3$-dimensional parallelepiped spanned by the columns of $A$ is depicted below:\n\n![](images/25/2597b48a010607fca71c551dbce8e9e2bbeda47d0892a582347ae1cdf19f1bf6.png)\n\nThe volume $V$ of this parallelepiped equals the absolute value of the determinant of $A{:}$\n\n$$\nV = \\left|\\det(A)\\right|\n$$\n\n- Now suppose the columns of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $B$ are ${\\color{black}\\mathbf{p}}$ and ${\\color{black}\\mathbf{q}}.$\n![](images/f5/f593c123eb081da0399e74c665325774758b48c5f086a7475b959905e1de2763.svg)\nThe $2$-dimensional parallelepiped (i.e., the parallelogram) spanned by vectors $\\mathbf{p}$ and $\\mathbf{q}$ is shown below:\n\n![](images/4a/4af1971404b188df75bb3d62d47ac95340c5e1791066e5358370d743230baa45.png)\n\nThe two-dimensional volume $V$ (i.e., the area) of this parallelepiped equals the absolute value of the determinant of $B{:}$\n\n$$\nV = \\left|\\det(B)\\right|\n$$\n\n- In general, the absolute value of the determinant of an $n\\times n$ matrix $M$ gives the $n$-dimensional volume of the parallelepiped spanned by the columns of $M.$\n\nIn this lesson, we studied the following property:\n\n*$\\det(A) = 0$ if and only if one of the columns of $A$ is a linear combination of other columns of $A$.*\n\nWhat does this property mean in geometric terms?\n\nThe component $\\det(A) = 0$ implies that the $n$-dimensional parallelepiped formed by the columns of $A$ has zero volume. Conversely, when one of the columns of $A$ is a linear combination of other columns, it indicates that the corresponding parallelepiped possesses at most $n-1$ distinct dimensions. \n\nConsider the following examples:\n\n- In the case of a $3$-dimensional parallelepiped, if the vector $\\mathbf{c}$ is a linear combination of the vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$, then $\\mathbf{c}$ lies within the plane containing $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$. In other words, the vectors $\\mathbf{a}$, $\\mathbf{b}$, and $\\mathbf{c}$ are coplanar. Consequently, we obtain a \"flat\" ($2$-dimensional) figure with a volume of $0$.\n\n- For the parallelogram example, if the vector $\\mathbf{q}$ is a linear combination of the vector $\\mathbf{p}$ (i.e., $\\mathbf{p}$ and $\\mathbf{q}$ are proportional), then $\\mathbf{q}$ resides on the line containing vector $\\mathbf{p}$. In other words, the vectors $\\mathbf{p}$ and $\\mathbf{q}$ are collinear. This results in a $1$-dimensional object with a $2$-dimensional volume (i.e., area) of $0$." :tutorial/id #uuid "1f7191c6-60ca-5c44-befc-8dbad7779b08" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6893 :tutorial/title "Geometric Interpretation of a Zero Determinant"} :step/id #uuid "8c5dbeea-b2e4-5fe9-9d8c-98750164b70c" :step/math-academy-id 41766 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134907}}] :activity/title "Conditions When a Determinant Equals Zero" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186134042}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046673} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415480} :activity/id #uuid "d3285aa6-5f09-548a-886c-a1acc2f7b8d6" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049312 :topic/id #uuid "eb826c2f-ae04-5de5-b6a6-7efcf7e974ae" :topic/math-academy-id 2201} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415480 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415477 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "022ff781-0bb6-5222-8917-bb9fe088d40f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415445} {:answer/id #uuid "68acb0f6-b335-5e47-b870-8fcc301ed02a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415455} {:answer/id #uuid "a31adf10-60d1-51b5-9022-8cf1940c42be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415461} {:answer/id #uuid "ebaffa84-7e8a-5edc-8432-6adf9e5b2b42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415465} {:answer/id #uuid "fae22a7a-c8b8-5a0b-bb2c-5bc815b3b48d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415467}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415445} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9ec239f-8a57-5ead-b4ff-042aa539477e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415472}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d4554f29-f5fb-5556-852c-2f9d95422a4d" :question/math-academy-id "q-42464" :question/problem "Find $5det(A)+7det(B)$, where \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & \\sqrt{3} & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 3\\sqrt{3} & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & \\sqrt{2} & 0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & 1 & 0 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ 4 & 3 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- Since $A$ has proportional rows, we obtain\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & \\sqrt{3} & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 3\\sqrt{3} & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟹\\,det(A)=0.\n$$\n\n- Since $B$ has a zero column, we obtain\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & \\sqrt{2} & 0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & 1 & 0 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ 4 & 3 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟹\\,det(B)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $5det(A)+7det(B)=5\\cdot 0+7\\cdot 0=0.$"} :step/id #uuid "0922e6fb-1dc5-5a88-a985-bdf2643cb465" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415484}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415481 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415479 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c54ada56-3e8b-562f-84dd-634b27dac9cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453904} {:answer/id #uuid "1bc835ea-e9b6-5ca5-a77e-0c3bd1af050b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453905}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453904} :answer-field/id #uuid "63c0a961-3dd4-5dcd-85e3-43b3a0493c55" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454702} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2456bbca-5dde-5fa3-a774-4b5dfb8431ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453906} {:answer/id #uuid "1b80c1f5-4c11-5a34-bb1a-faf5cdfce42f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453907}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453906} :answer-field/id #uuid "36456e08-b5e0-5ffb-8bc0-2e13784267ad" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454703} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e336f47-61c7-5dfa-b071-bc593d519e33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453908} {:answer/id #uuid "05d59b93-9de5-5bdc-af50-0154b6214595" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453909}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453909} :answer-field/id #uuid "0bb3bd4f-a1b2-5b23-a316-a0c1d1cd0bc3" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454704}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "f8a11ae3-b740-5975-a251-a4796d69b742" :question/math-academy-id "q-43307" :question/problem "Let $A$ be a $3\\times 3$ matrix such that $|A|=0.$ Which of the following statements must be true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $A$ has a zero column\n- **II.** $A$ has two proportional rows\n- **III.** One of the rows/columns of $A$ is a linear combination of other rows/columns of $A$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider our statements in turn.\n\n- Statements I and II are false. For example, consider the matrix \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nExpending across the $1$st column, we have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A| & =1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix}+1\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(3-2)+(1-2) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nbut $A$ has neither a zero column nor two proportional rows.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, we can make use of the following fact:\n\n$|A|=0$ *if and only if one of the rows/columns of $A$ is a linear combination of other rows/columns of $A.$*\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."} :step/id #uuid "73a434b3-1e72-5102-a3b4-f3e485e5088a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415482}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415482 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415475 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "045918a8-9cd8-5a07-b550-ae46024d9091" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$|A|$ and $|C|$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415446} {:answer/id #uuid "402ee237-56af-5ed0-b68b-15a4eb93a4c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$|B|$ and $|C|$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415451} {:answer/id #uuid "82c9a685-f3be-5652-9e92-444a23e21632" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$|A|$ and $|B|$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415458} {:answer/id #uuid "f5705fa2-288d-561a-9ae0-435e05884593" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$|A|,$ $|B|,$ and $|C|$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415466} {:answer/id #uuid "fff98d8e-e1e9-59ce-9f45-62d4096f2b19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$|C|$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415469}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415466} :answer-field/id #uuid "3898e8c8-305f-5107-8e55-3fa0057b225c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415470}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "135c5d03-e391-5a5b-8227-7d54c7203318" :question/math-academy-id "q-140048" :question/problem "Which of the following determinants are equal to $0?$\n\n$$\n|A|=|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}|,\\,|B|=|\\begin{aligned}1 & \\sqrt{2} & 1 \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & 2 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ -1 & \\sqrt{2} & 1\\end{aligned}|,\\,|C|=|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}|\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the given determinants in turn.\n\n- Since $A$ is a triangular matrix, its determinant is given by the product of the elements of the main diagonal:\n\n$$\n|A|=0\\cdot 0\\cdot 0\\cdot 0=0\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ has proportional rows: \n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & \\sqrt{2} & 1 \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & 2 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ -1 & \\sqrt{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n \nAs a result, $|B|=0.$\n\n- The matrix $C$ has a zero column: \n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nAs a result, $|C|=0.$\n\nTherefore, $|A|,$ $|B|,$ and $|C|$ are equal to $0.$"} :step/id #uuid "7fde0eea-1a95-5e36-bc1d-53171442da99"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415483 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415478 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1859d7c5-0d55-5273-a004-f8368886466b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415448} {:answer/id #uuid "4a61a420-d52d-5f1a-b48b-ade03dffdca3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415453} {:answer/id #uuid "5fcc61bd-b9ee-5f9e-8558-651f8ea9c858" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415454} {:answer/id #uuid "851dc3ec-467b-568b-a964-3b5e34b3527e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415459} {:answer/id #uuid "e2e4e722-717d-508c-a98d-112f70b09d9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415463}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415463} :answer-field/id #uuid "f920ea7f-06a7-52e9-8586-d0e6b44af6b3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415474}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e897ce2c-45dd-5cf3-9d25-ca84c0dd6ea8" :question/math-academy-id "q-42881" :question/problem "Find $3det(A)-det(B)$, where \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0.57 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- Since $A$ is a triangular matrix, we have\n\n$$\ndet(A)=2\\cdot 1\\cdot 3=6.\n$$\n\n- Since $B$ has proportional columns, we obtain\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0.57 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟹\\,det(B)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $3det(A)-det(B)=3\\cdot 6-0=18.$"} :step/id #uuid "8a73fe86-1dcc-5158-b5d0-cfa5bf0ca01e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415481}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415484 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415476 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "071ee791-8aa1-59a9-829e-f23787b55570" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473130} {:answer/id #uuid "be789ad4-7b1c-517c-b647-7e02fbdc4606" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473131}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186473131} :answer-field/id #uuid "1c846e82-5642-55c2-bd6c-bf9a65cc14b7" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473960} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "380f5ebb-1b0a-524b-8107-7d50c50a8d0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473132} {:answer/id #uuid "2adc7349-dd06-5e86-bb35-5bbbb5b04583" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473133}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186473132} :answer-field/id #uuid "37aa9389-4822-5c22-ad9b-bf758d46df45" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473961} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c2322edf-ee14-5f20-8831-ec7cadfc1dd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473134} {:answer/id #uuid "d8ca5be3-1fbc-5f41-bcbb-41b53ef5da56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473135}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186473135} :answer-field/id #uuid "873d7824-ac7f-5ce0-ad45-49263a39fd15" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473962}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8d883981-f066-5218-a1bf-dd76574de46e" :question/math-academy-id "q-42469" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2.05 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & \\sqrt{7} & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix given above. Identify **all** of the reasons why one can conclude that $|A|=0?$\n- **I.** $A$ has proportional columns\n- **II.** $A$ has identical rows\n- **III.** $A$ has a zero row\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- $A$ has proportional columns: \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2.05 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & \\sqrt{7} & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,✓\n$$\n\n- $A$ does *not* have identical rows.\n\n- $A$ has a zero row: \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2.05 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & \\sqrt{7} & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,✓\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "a995ede1-d1f3-53d5-97cb-aed29dce50f7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415483}}] :activity/title "Conditions When a Determinant Equals Zero" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415444}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416116} :activity/id #uuid "9cb6054b-7ed5-5992-92b3-318764a5074c" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049312 :topic/id #uuid "eb826c2f-ae04-5de5-b6a6-7efcf7e974ae" :topic/math-academy-id 2201} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416114 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415477 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "022ff781-0bb6-5222-8917-bb9fe088d40f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415445} {:answer/id #uuid "68acb0f6-b335-5e47-b870-8fcc301ed02a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415455} {:answer/id #uuid "a31adf10-60d1-51b5-9022-8cf1940c42be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415461} {:answer/id #uuid "ebaffa84-7e8a-5edc-8432-6adf9e5b2b42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415465} {:answer/id #uuid "fae22a7a-c8b8-5a0b-bb2c-5bc815b3b48d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415467}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415445} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9ec239f-8a57-5ead-b4ff-042aa539477e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415472}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d4554f29-f5fb-5556-852c-2f9d95422a4d" :question/math-academy-id "q-42464" :question/problem "Find $5det(A)+7det(B)$, where \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & \\sqrt{3} & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 3\\sqrt{3} & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & \\sqrt{2} & 0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & 1 & 0 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ 4 & 3 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- Since $A$ has proportional rows, we obtain\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & \\sqrt{3} & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 3\\sqrt{3} & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟹\\,det(A)=0.\n$$\n\n- Since $B$ has a zero column, we obtain\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & \\sqrt{2} & 0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & 1 & 0 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ 4 & 3 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟹\\,det(B)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $5det(A)+7det(B)=5\\cdot 0+7\\cdot 0=0.$"} :step/id #uuid "21677cec-5329-5c17-ae68-e4ce9c1eced7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416118}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416115 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272012 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "643b4a3a-7263-51ad-8b17-fdd82678cf99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464084} {:answer/id #uuid "e3e66fb8-1a18-56f4-8033-3baba4940db9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464085}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186464084} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ceb200f-e369-571b-b619-8f16a603488c" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464976} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "198009c8-d9e6-556e-aa1c-228a37b12311" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464086} {:answer/id #uuid "da19cf9c-0b56-5a20-b004-c70d313f5184" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464087}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186464087} :answer-field/id #uuid "d98905ac-4493-5551-96b2-6d52f8e3f566" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464977} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "63ea76cf-d936-573e-a0be-56de7954b8ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464088} {:answer/id #uuid "24e2aa19-faca-5a81-a238-e9e984e89ef4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464089}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186464089} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed42e6d2-1aff-5708-ad8c-b45de0195450" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464978}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "f60fdb9d-cf89-59bd-ad65-e9ec096b82df" :question/math-academy-id "q-140049" :question/problem "Let $A$ be a $2\\times 2$ matrix such that $|A|\\ne 0.$ Which of the following statements must be true?\n- **I.** $A$ is a triangular matrix\n- **II.** $A$ does not have identical rows\n- **III.** $A$ does not have proportional columns\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Let's consider our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, consider the matrix \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe have that \n\n$$\n|A|=1\\cdot 1-(-2)\\cdot 2=5\\ne 0\n$$\n\nbut $A$ is not a triangular matrix.\n\n- Statements II and III are true. Indeed, we can make use of the following fact:\n\n$|A|=0$ *if and only if one of the rows/columns of $A$ is a linear combination of other rows/columns of $A.$*\n\nIn the case of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, this means that one of the rows/columns must be proportional to another row/column.\nHence, if $|A|\\ne 0$ then $A$ doesn't have proportional nor identical rows/columns.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"."} :step/id #uuid "5d4a636e-e59e-599e-aff6-d3384708bbf3"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416116 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329652 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ecd3639-37c9-5252-aa12-89458787a945" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467506} {:answer/id #uuid "4a17d94f-af1d-51db-b4a1-c3f5d3f929bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467507}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186467506} :answer-field/id #uuid "bdf24f07-c421-5ff2-bb73-41cf2a1bb824" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468474} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d6ede3e0-89dd-5960-bddd-720db62f0e98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467508} {:answer/id #uuid "9298449a-ad9c-56e2-ac99-09c0dc4fccc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467509}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186467509} :answer-field/id #uuid "c910b60c-d032-56a5-9324-9976088368c8" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468475} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b966358-ec55-5272-a99d-1379768d11ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467510} {:answer/id #uuid "7421f4b3-99e2-5360-a4df-4fcdc8cb3bc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467511}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186467511} :answer-field/id #uuid "5de5adf3-aa9f-52a9-9f7f-305d17cf782d" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468476}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "fffc6699-6d6e-50bb-8cc3-7d4f0e3f1d17" :question/math-academy-id "q-42460" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{5} & 1 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 2 & 4 \\\\ \\sqrt{5} & 1 & 5 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 4 & -1 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n \nConsider the matrix given above. Identify **all** of the reasons why one can conclude that $|A|=0?$\n- **I.** $A$ has a zero row\n- **II.** $A$ has two proportional columns\n- **III.** $A$ has two identical rows\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- $A$ does *not* have a zero row.\n\n- $A$ has two proportional columns:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{5} & 1 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 2 & 4 \\\\ \\sqrt{5} & 1 & 5 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 4 & -1 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\\,✓\n$$\n\n- $A$ has two identical rows: \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{5} & 1 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 2 & 4 \\\\ \\sqrt{5} & 1 & 5 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 4 & -1 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\\,✓\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "72236d0c-8f54-552b-be75-f74f2ec50cbd" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416114}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416117 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415479 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c54ada56-3e8b-562f-84dd-634b27dac9cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453904} {:answer/id #uuid "1bc835ea-e9b6-5ca5-a77e-0c3bd1af050b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453905}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453904} :answer-field/id #uuid "63c0a961-3dd4-5dcd-85e3-43b3a0493c55" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454702} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2456bbca-5dde-5fa3-a774-4b5dfb8431ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453906} {:answer/id #uuid "1b80c1f5-4c11-5a34-bb1a-faf5cdfce42f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453907}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453906} :answer-field/id #uuid "36456e08-b5e0-5ffb-8bc0-2e13784267ad" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454703} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e336f47-61c7-5dfa-b071-bc593d519e33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453908} {:answer/id #uuid "05d59b93-9de5-5bdc-af50-0154b6214595" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453909}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453909} :answer-field/id #uuid "0bb3bd4f-a1b2-5b23-a316-a0c1d1cd0bc3" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454704}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "f8a11ae3-b740-5975-a251-a4796d69b742" :question/math-academy-id "q-43307" :question/problem "Let $A$ be a $3\\times 3$ matrix such that $|A|=0.$ Which of the following statements must be true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $A$ has a zero column\n- **II.** $A$ has two proportional rows\n- **III.** One of the rows/columns of $A$ is a linear combination of other rows/columns of $A$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider our statements in turn.\n\n- Statements I and II are false. For example, consider the matrix \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nExpending across the $1$st column, we have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A| & =1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix}+1\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(3-2)+(1-2) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nbut $A$ has neither a zero column nor two proportional rows.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, we can make use of the following fact:\n\n$|A|=0$ *if and only if one of the rows/columns of $A$ is a linear combination of other rows/columns of $A.$*\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."} :step/id #uuid "cd1aa3f6-63e2-5747-ab7a-9aca6a4ae1ae" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416115}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416118 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402861 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "78b84216-c188-5769-a0cf-2f3c4b5d4fe1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$|B|$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402863} {:answer/id #uuid "47a2ba38-2156-5dd9-922d-70e7a9112796" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$|A|$ and $|C|$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402864} {:answer/id #uuid "bf232768-62b0-5a99-b6ae-c2f1d1ac4e96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$|C|$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402865} {:answer/id #uuid "5c171a58-43ca-5ac8-896f-be2e38df60c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$|A|$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402866} {:answer/id #uuid "75bb085b-9153-5df0-9e32-3ce23b5de8fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$|A|$ and $|B|$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402867}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402863} :answer-field/id #uuid "a4619bd4-0fd6-5b0e-85d8-4d713cc4997e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402862}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b44f28c6-ca93-5246-be2b-1a2725d36e07" :question/math-academy-id "q-43057" :question/problem "Which of the following determinants are equal to $0?$\n\n$$\n|A|=|\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & -6 & 0 \\\\ \\sqrt{5} & 8 & 2\\end{aligned}|,\\,|B|=|\\begin{aligned}1 & \\sqrt{2} & 4 \\\\ 2 & 2\\sqrt{2} & 8 \\\\ 0 & -\\sqrt{3} & 4\\end{aligned}|,\\,|C|=|\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\sqrt{3} & 4 \\\\ 0 & 3 & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}|.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the given determinants in turn.\n\n- The matrix $A$ is a triangular matrix, hence \n\n$$\n|A|=3\\cdot (-6)\\cdot 2=-36\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ has proportional rows: \n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & \\sqrt{2} & 4 \\\\ 2 & 2\\sqrt{2} & 8 \\\\ 0 & -\\sqrt{3} & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nAs a result, $|B|=0.$\n\n- The matrix $C$ is a triangular matrix, hence \n\n$$\n|C|=(-1)\\cdot 3\\cdot 4=-12\\ne 0\n$$\n\nTherefore, only $|B|$ is equal to $0.$"} :step/id #uuid "dd405466-fc10-5821-904a-fe28c7027da8" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416117}}] :activity/title "Conditions When a Determinant Equals Zero" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416113}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427638} :activity/id #uuid "831bdc32-0d6c-55b8-aa0e-17d1809789f3" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049312 :topic/id #uuid "eb826c2f-ae04-5de5-b6a6-7efcf7e974ae" :topic/math-academy-id 2201} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427637 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415475 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "045918a8-9cd8-5a07-b550-ae46024d9091" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$|A|$ and $|C|$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415446} {:answer/id #uuid "402ee237-56af-5ed0-b68b-15a4eb93a4c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$|B|$ and $|C|$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415451} {:answer/id #uuid "82c9a685-f3be-5652-9e92-444a23e21632" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$|A|$ and $|B|$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415458} {:answer/id #uuid "f5705fa2-288d-561a-9ae0-435e05884593" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$|A|,$ $|B|,$ and $|C|$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415466} {:answer/id #uuid "fff98d8e-e1e9-59ce-9f45-62d4096f2b19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$|C|$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415469}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415466} :answer-field/id #uuid "3898e8c8-305f-5107-8e55-3fa0057b225c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415470}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "135c5d03-e391-5a5b-8227-7d54c7203318" :question/math-academy-id "q-140048" :question/problem "Which of the following determinants are equal to $0?$\n\n$$\n|A|=|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}|,\\,|B|=|\\begin{aligned}1 & \\sqrt{2} & 1 \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & 2 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ -1 & \\sqrt{2} & 1\\end{aligned}|,\\,|C|=|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}|\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the given determinants in turn.\n\n- Since $A$ is a triangular matrix, its determinant is given by the product of the elements of the main diagonal:\n\n$$\n|A|=0\\cdot 0\\cdot 0\\cdot 0=0\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ has proportional rows: \n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & \\sqrt{2} & 1 \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & 2 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ -1 & \\sqrt{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n \nAs a result, $|B|=0.$\n\n- The matrix $C$ has a zero column: \n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nAs a result, $|C|=0.$\n\nTherefore, $|A|,$ $|B|,$ and $|C|$ are equal to $0.$"} :step/id #uuid "1a88a749-d24f-53f0-b27d-bb6b30fd5b09"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427638 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329653 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a7a447b8-7f09-5270-aca4-b17f9770d76a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467512} {:answer/id #uuid "20bcc14b-7336-54e8-a261-0dbcc7509b69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467513}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186467513} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b661764-dcf8-5eba-bedc-e2374ffb5a83" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468477} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5f27d7c7-943c-56e2-9e1a-eb7154c900ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467514} {:answer/id #uuid "9113697f-b704-5925-b69c-e204a21d502a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467515}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186467514} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba64ddd8-bbc6-577f-b73b-e524ed587e49" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468478} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "68448d61-0ddf-531f-8009-f672e592a9b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467516} {:answer/id #uuid "84b46424-4893-5f87-bbc8-ae085db91a24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467517}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186467516} :answer-field/id #uuid "b2a724bd-e000-5d5f-b930-fed8c566efc4" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468479}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "59d4edee-acd9-59e0-80f8-ec390e2e0ee0" :question/math-academy-id "q-42465" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 8 & 2 & 6 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & \\sqrt{6} & 9 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 4 & \\sqrt{5}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n \n\nConsider the matrix given above. Identify **all** of the reasons why one can conclude that $|A|=0?$\n\nI. $A$ has proportional rows\n\nII. $A$ has identical columns\n\nIII. $A$ has a zero column\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- $A$ has proportional rows: \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 8 & 2 & 6 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & \\sqrt{6} & 9 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 4 & \\sqrt{5}\\end{bmatrix}\\,✓\n$$\n\n- $A$ does *not* have identical columns.\n\n- $A$ does *not* have a zero column.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} :step/id #uuid "388fa099-949a-55ed-903a-cb5e3105da1f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427639}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427639 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134407 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ba7d7bc9-77ef-583c-8caa-22315aad7aca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$|A|$, $|B|$, and $|C|$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392523} {:answer/id #uuid "23f02ed7-eb83-5988-a1ca-ee80eac659ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$|B|$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392524} {:answer/id #uuid "d767aec8-a44c-5175-bbcd-b71f577015fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$|A|$ and $|C|$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392525} {:answer/id #uuid "ee7ae539-44fc-5d03-b8b9-5a5b498db62e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$|A|$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392526} {:answer/id #uuid "3737a2a5-a57d-571a-a651-f2cad4ee3049" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$|A|$ and $|B|$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392527}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392525} :answer-field/id #uuid "00ef10bc-f305-5726-8271-949923025e38" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392522}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "cee2c526-7176-535a-81f7-173bfaefecec" :question/math-academy-id "q-42882" :question/problem "Which of the following determinants are equal to $0?$\n\n$$\n|A|=|\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\sqrt[5]{2} & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}|,\\,|B|=|\\begin{aligned}1 & \\sqrt{2} & 4 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}|,\\,|C|=|\\begin{aligned}1 & \\sqrt{2} & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 8 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}|\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the given determinants in turn.\n\n- The matrix $A$ has a zero row: \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\sqrt[5]{2} & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nAs a result, $|A|=0.$\n\n- The matrix $B$ is a triangular matrix, hence \n\n$$\n|B|=1\\cdot 3\\cdot 4=12\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- The matrix $C$ has proportional columns: \n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & \\sqrt{2} & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 8 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nAs a result, $|C|=0.$\n\nTherefore, only $|A|$ and $|C|$ are equal to $0.$"} :step/id #uuid "38a574a5-5c5f-57c3-888b-5d032802d120" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427640}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427640 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134414 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8cc3eb36-fa25-5b0c-a9f4-d0d9b6fd431c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448644} {:answer/id #uuid "024de02f-c585-5ff1-ae1f-906a7964b6c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448645}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186448644} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4ba6b54-8b9e-5c38-a842-718279128c22" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449362} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a8759331-0ea5-5e82-a056-c79e5438af33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448646} {:answer/id #uuid "e2f5cff8-7a78-538b-955f-a82c9351731b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448647}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186448647} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae83d106-f07c-5d04-9fd2-5832bd202f49" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449363} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "318ceaba-c9a3-5098-81fd-4d438f7912e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448648} {:answer/id #uuid "773df97a-4a06-5ac8-b5d2-9731cc8bba08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448649}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186448648} :answer-field/id #uuid "93e5158f-ae92-576e-abfb-6ad450c72c06" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449364}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "ef67ff18-5c70-5c00-bdf0-27b79dc90b72" :question/math-academy-id "q-43306" :question/problem "Let $A$ be a $2\\times 2$ matrix such that $|A|=0.$ Which of the following statements must be true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $A$ has two identical rows\n\n- **II.** One of the rows/columns of $A$ is proportional to another row/column of $A$\n\n- **III.** $A$ has two identical columns" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider our statements in turn.\n\n- Statements I and III are false. For example, consider the matrix \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 8\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe have that \n\n$$\n|A|=1\\cdot 8-4\\cdot 2=0\n$$\n\nbut $A$ has neither two identical rows nor two identical columns.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we can make use of the following fact:\n\n$|A|=0$ *if and only if one of the rows/columns of $A$ is a linear combination of other rows/columns of $A.$*\n\nIn the case of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, this means that one of the rows/columns must be proportional to another row/column.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."} :step/id #uuid "9758fa31-2a83-569c-bfa7-a2c551b1e63f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427641}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427641 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427636 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c03a16b-9dee-5cff-a0ff-6bb3df36fd82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427630} {:answer/id #uuid "317dd47a-723e-5f7b-bb84-76a98d476a7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427631} {:answer/id #uuid "51f27d1e-2a2e-57fb-a28c-b54b35283c0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427632} {:answer/id #uuid "a64fab7a-ae4b-5ae6-9ea8-75c59aee1472" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427633} {:answer/id #uuid "c7ae2b06-54e1-5537-a7a1-e4af212bb060" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427634}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427630} :answer-field/id #uuid "25bbbab1-f79f-5a4a-95b4-1d90cbb77585" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427635}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "df55035e-2530-507b-9e8e-793ed8f0bafc" :question/math-academy-id "q-82284" :question/problem "Find $-4det(A)+3det(B)$, where \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & \\sqrt{3} & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2} & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{2} & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- Since $A$ has a zero column, we obtain\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & \\sqrt{2} & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟹\\,det(A)=0.\n$$\n\n- Since $B$ is a triangular matrix, its determinant is given by the product of the elements of the main diagonal: \n\n$$\ndet(B)=\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot 3=6\n$$\n\nTherefore, $-4det(A)+3det(B)=-4\\cdot 0+3\\cdot 6=18.$"} :step/id #uuid "f880731a-4b09-56ca-8e32-d2d537e03030" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427637}}] :activity/title "Conditions When a Determinant Equals Zero" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427629}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499619} :activity/id #uuid "7116892c-7fc3-5b1b-aa93-d2dfc9d1e89e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049312 :topic/id #uuid "eb826c2f-ae04-5de5-b6a6-7efcf7e974ae" :topic/math-academy-id 2201} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499596 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134414 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8cc3eb36-fa25-5b0c-a9f4-d0d9b6fd431c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448644} {:answer/id #uuid "024de02f-c585-