urn.\n\n- Statements I and III are false. For example, consider the matrix \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 8\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe have that \n\n$$\n|A|=1\\cdot 8-4\\cdot 2=0\n$$\n\nbut $A$ has neither two identical rows nor two identical columns.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we can make use of the following fact:\n\n$|A|=0$ *if and only if one of the rows/columns of $A$ is a linear combination of other rows/columns of $A.$*\n\nIn the case of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, this means that one of the rows/columns must be proportional to another row/column.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."} :step/id #uuid "72bae4bc-a32a-5255-ba7a-a875edc0b957" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499606}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499602 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415477 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "022ff781-0bb6-5222-8917-bb9fe088d40f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415445} {:answer/id #uuid "68acb0f6-b335-5e47-b870-8fcc301ed02a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415455} {:answer/id #uuid "a31adf10-60d1-51b5-9022-8cf1940c42be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415461} {:answer/id #uuid "ebaffa84-7e8a-5edc-8432-6adf9e5b2b42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415465} {:answer/id #uuid "fae22a7a-c8b8-5a0b-bb2c-5bc815b3b48d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415467}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415445} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9ec239f-8a57-5ead-b4ff-042aa539477e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415472}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d4554f29-f5fb-5556-852c-2f9d95422a4d" :question/math-academy-id "q-42464" :question/problem "Find $5det(A)+7det(B)$, where \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & \\sqrt{3} & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 3\\sqrt{3} & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & \\sqrt{2} & 0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & 1 & 0 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ 4 & 3 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- Since $A$ has proportional rows, we obtain\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & \\sqrt{3} & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 3\\sqrt{3} & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟹\\,det(A)=0.\n$$\n\n- Since $B$ has a zero column, we obtain\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & \\sqrt{2} & 0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & 1 & 0 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ 4 & 3 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟹\\,det(B)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $5det(A)+7det(B)=5\\cdot 0+7\\cdot 0=0.$"} :step/id #uuid "96688804-9fe5-58e3-852b-6c7c9c3bf052" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499596}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499606 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272012 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "643b4a3a-7263-51ad-8b17-fdd82678cf99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464084} {:answer/id #uuid "e3e66fb8-1a18-56f4-8033-3baba4940db9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464085}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186464084} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ceb200f-e369-571b-b619-8f16a603488c" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464976} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "198009c8-d9e6-556e-aa1c-228a37b12311" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464086} {:answer/id #uuid "da19cf9c-0b56-5a20-b004-c70d313f5184" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464087}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186464087} :answer-field/id #uuid "d98905ac-4493-5551-96b2-6d52f8e3f566" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464977} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "63ea76cf-d936-573e-a0be-56de7954b8ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464088} {:answer/id #uuid "24e2aa19-faca-5a81-a238-e9e984e89ef4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464089}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186464089} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed42e6d2-1aff-5708-ad8c-b45de0195450" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464978}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "f60fdb9d-cf89-59bd-ad65-e9ec096b82df" :question/math-academy-id "q-140049" :question/problem "Let $A$ be a $2\\times 2$ matrix such that $|A|\\ne 0.$ Which of the following statements must be true?\n- **I.** $A$ is a triangular matrix\n- **II.** $A$ does not have identical rows\n- **III.** $A$ does not have proportional columns\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Let's consider our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, consider the matrix \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe have that \n\n$$\n|A|=1\\cdot 1-(-2)\\cdot 2=5\\ne 0\n$$\n\nbut $A$ is not a triangular matrix.\n\n- Statements II and III are true. Indeed, we can make use of the following fact:\n\n$|A|=0$ *if and only if one of the rows/columns of $A$ is a linear combination of other rows/columns of $A.$*\n\nIn the case of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, this means that one of the rows/columns must be proportional to another row/column.\nHence, if $|A|\\ne 0$ then $A$ doesn't have proportional nor identical rows/columns.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"."} :step/id #uuid "9b5040f7-fe09-55f4-9bb3-3899218d89d2"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499619 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134393 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d65d5f6-e3b7-5ee9-95cf-58c3e498a702" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459975} {:answer/id #uuid "646a048e-f630-5013-95fe-6659b566a35f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459976}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459976} :answer-field/id #uuid "34d3f262-127e-5585-b97c-20a126667044" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460854} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8460f2bb-e253-5210-9218-4e96e796beae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459977} {:answer/id #uuid "40f38a8d-b50e-505f-9411-5989253d0642" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459978}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459977} :answer-field/id #uuid "cde9df91-1435-51c4-9805-9c117768203b" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460855} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d90e2fb7-b1da-50a5-82da-f4e30f796fcc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459979} {:answer/id #uuid "8df48ba2-f889-595c-9590-a8404cf9b0b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459980}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459980} :answer-field/id #uuid "f677a744-3bd0-564b-ade4-affd6abcf2a2" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460856}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2d571c31-8e0e-520c-b34f-482cf5518003" :question/math-academy-id "q-42458" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix given above. Identify **all** of the reasons why one can conclude that $|A|=0?$\n- **I.** $A$ has a zero column\n- **II.** $A$ has identical columns\n- **III.** $A$ has identical rows\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- $A$ has a zero column: \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\,✓\n$$\n\n- $A$ does *not* have identical columns.\n\n- $A$ has identical rows: \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\,✓\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "c48a0407-2623-5e3c-b4d7-9d37ae7f101c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499627}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499627 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134394 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4731d43a-1037-5467-97a5-e798a96ef9d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327555} {:answer/id #uuid "658a8d30-d9af-5e26-83da-9719b1081d76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327818} {:answer/id #uuid "6d1056e6-0216-54a8-a348-f7fd16cd261c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327879} {:answer/id #uuid "cb4f71df-9ae0-539d-bc37-b9dfa1a2efd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328715} {:answer/id #uuid "f61627d1-d9a7-5fa0-8601-a797fc2e5d46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329093}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329093} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ecac406-0aef-584c-9d48-0a6c47d439e8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329307}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a804e446-7433-50c7-a8ce-2769d7a93237" :question/math-academy-id "q-42459" :question/problem "Find $4det(A)+4det(B)$, where \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2\\sqrt{2} & 1 \\\\ 1 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & \\sqrt{2} & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- For the matrix $A,$ we have\n\n$$\ndet(A)=2\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}-1\\cdot 1=3.\n$$\n\n- Since $B$ has proportional columns, we obtain\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & \\sqrt{2} & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟹\\,det(B)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $4det(A)+4det(B)=4\\cdot 3+4\\cdot 0=12.$"} :step/id #uuid "e27b814b-a73a-54cb-83e5-018345109fb2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499602}}] :activity/title "Conditions When a Determinant Equals Zero" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498725}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 1080.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125406} :activity/id #uuid "9ba6f288-55b6-582e-8aa1-d15ec442bd9c" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050376 :topic/id #uuid "44312a5e-e7e0-507f-86f6-c3bcdecfd7fe" :topic/math-academy-id 1964} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125406 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125785 :tutorial/content "We can find the eigenvalues of a $3\\times 3$ matrix in much the same way as for $2\\!\\times\\! 2$ matrices. \n\nRemember, $\\lambda$ is an eigenvalue of $A$ if $A\\mathbf{v} = \\lambda\\mathbf{v}$ for some non-zero vector $\\mathbf{v}$. This implies that the equation\n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nhas a non-trivial solution, which means that the matrix $A-\\lambda I$ is singular, and consequently\n$\\textrm{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0.$\nTherefore, if we want to find the eigenvalues of a $n\\!\\times\\! n$ matrix $A,$ we have to find the solutions of the characteristic equation\n\n$$\n\\textrm{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0.\n$$\n\nLet's go through this process in the case of the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n![](images/21/21e0994519fbba32a84020ce4661e98600c1307947083ddc3afe9902658143c1.svg)\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix $A-\\lambda I{:}$\n\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}\\lambda  & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\lambda \\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2-\\lambda  & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5-\\lambda \\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set and solve the characteristic equation $\\,\\textrm{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0.$ Using the fact that the determinant of a triangular (or diagonal) matrix is given by the product of the elements on the main diagonal, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}-2-\\lambda  & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-2-\\lambda )(-8-\\lambda )(5-\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)(\\lambda +8)(\\lambda -5) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are $\\lambda=-2,$ $\\lambda=-8,$ and $\\lambda=5.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "535c5b8b-f815-5ded-8ae5-668bac420af7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3564 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "9f05d37d-484a-5ae0-8ed9-0b46584f4a7f" :step/math-academy-id 20093 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125407}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125407 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124522 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124331 :question/id #uuid "4b2fcf5d-0da3-5676-b4c6-54095c3fb45c" :question/math-academy-id "e-5486"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "289ae874-f555-50ee-896f-eb2188004124" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049159} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050001}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125012} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125017} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323857} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323904} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323909} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400881} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427385} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447470} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447472}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Eigenvalues of a 3x3 Matrix Containing a Block of Zeros"} :step/id #uuid "9edf1314-60a8-581e-afc3-f39ecd8b7951" :step/math-academy-id 20094 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125408}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125408 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125786 :tutorial/content "The **algebraic multiplicity** of an eigenvalue $\\lambda$ is the number of times it is a solution of the characteristic equation. \n\nFor example, consider a $6\\times 6$ matrix $A$ with the following characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n(\\lambda-{\\color{red}4})(\\lambda-{\\color{red}4})(\\lambda-1)(\\lambda+8)^{\\color{blue}3} =0\n$$\n\nThe eigenvalue $\\lambda={\\color{red}4}$ is a double root. Therefore, it has algebraic multiplicity $2.$ \n\nBy the same reasoning, we say that \n\n- $\\lambda=1$ has algebraic multiplicity $1,$ and\n\n- $\\lambda=-8$ has algebraic multiplicity $\\color{blue}3$." :tutorial/id #uuid "db18f185-0dce-547f-95f2-71b60a6d4d2b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3565 :tutorial/title "The Algebraic Multiplicity of an Eigenvalue"} :step/id #uuid "37883812-d28d-5bd5-97af-2df4b9f73f66" :step/math-academy-id 20095 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125488}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125409 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124523 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124332 :question/id #uuid "76c52a74-9b98-5df5-ac63-64e92af1a580" :question/math-academy-id "e-5487"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5abea3ab-76a7-5598-8ede-b4cf88dd47d7" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050429} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051061}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125013} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125016} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272116} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323910} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323911} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366013} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366518} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404128} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404378} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442272} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492037}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Eigenvalues of a General 3x3 Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "d8240de1-8637-5d84-886a-269d69011445" :step/math-academy-id 20096} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125488 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124571 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124380 :question/id #uuid "a1c51187-c0a6-505c-bc58-2319b439086e" :question/math-academy-id "e-7047"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef311af0-0e95-5574-a151-2971ad66bd08" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048505} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048669}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125011} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125062} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272092} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272102} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323856} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354507} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366006} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366365} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366511} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442271} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447465} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447473}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Eigenvalues of a 4x4 Matrix Containing a Block of Zeros"} :step/id #uuid "1c9b6469-fe8d-5b75-bb95-efbf2684e9ce" :step/math-academy-id 25951 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125409}}] :activity/title "The Characteristic Equation of a Matrix" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124749}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442276} :activity/id #uuid "3b7302f6-4f44-5e74-82d2-2ea10325bde3" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050376 :topic/id #uuid "44312a5e-e7e0-507f-86f6-c3bcdecfd7fe" :topic/math-academy-id 1964} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442273 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272116 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "343afa09-d3de-5a19-916a-586f7a7d1e10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\lambda }^{3}-2{\\lambda }^{2}-13\\lambda -30=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269747} {:answer/id #uuid "486477e7-c24f-5198-8b45-bd30ea74890e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\lambda }^{3}+2{\\lambda }^{2}+13\\lambda -15=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269929} {:answer/id #uuid "6228aba2-8afa-597c-95c4-f06c42a62e0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\lambda }^{3}+13{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270132} {:answer/id #uuid "d6af2f78-c6df-57d2-ac96-1c55af121e04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\lambda }^{3}+2{\\lambda }^{2}-13\\lambda +10=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271152} {:answer/id #uuid "f703200a-d0b5-505b-8a01-4c910b8e7ab9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\lambda }^{3}-2{\\lambda }^{2}-13\\lambda +25=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271436}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186271152} :answer-field/id #uuid "d819e362-fc4f-51eb-a589-2bd16ee43e88" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271891}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "29164807-d381-53a4-8efb-e16c83f907bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-140515" :question/problem "Find the characteristic equation of the matrix \n$C=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the matrix $\\,C-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nC-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}3-\\lambda  & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3-\\lambda  & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -2-\\lambda \\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation is $det(C-\\lambda I)=0.$ \n\nExpanding the determinant across the $1$st row, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(C-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}3-\\lambda  & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3-\\lambda  & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -2-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )\\begin{vmatrix}-3-\\lambda  & 3 \\\\ 2 & -2-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix}-(-1)\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix}+(-1)\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -3-\\lambda  \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}+5\\lambda )+(-\\lambda -5)-(5+\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ (-{\\lambda }^{3}-2{\\lambda }^{2}+15\\lambda )-\\lambda -5-5-\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ -{\\lambda }^{3}-2{\\lambda }^{2}+13\\lambda -10 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{3}+2{\\lambda }^{2}-13\\lambda +10 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "3399fe8e-8176-5e35-93d5-9a34329ea9c3"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442274 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442271 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0069a32c-24a8-592d-aebc-701756d8bdcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442259} {:answer/id #uuid "8e52e58b-8a75-5be5-b33c-025f9395fdc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442264} {:answer/id #uuid "95f7c606-fabc-51e1-8528-9632cdd50f62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442265} {:answer/id #uuid "c80a4f86-8e3e-57b3-9a46-45e60e433bf6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442267} {:answer/id #uuid "f29dc51e-2c92-5c6d-8ac3-24dd5e961743" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442268}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442265} :answer-field/id #uuid "24804dcb-504d-588f-82a7-fb6fe2bd5e63" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442269}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "970b7afa-4ca1-5d4a-a4eb-b7279227d558" :question/math-academy-id "q-37356" :question/problem "$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $B$ given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\lambda =-5$ is an eigenvalue of $B$\n\nII. $\\lambda =-1$ is an eigenvalue of $B$\n\nIII. The eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3$ has algebraic multiplicity $2$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the matrix $\\,B-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nB-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}-5-\\lambda  & 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3-\\lambda  & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -3-\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 & -1-\\lambda \\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation is $det(B-\\lambda I)=0.$ \n\nNotice that we can expand the $4\\times 4$ determinant across the $1$st column, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}-5-\\lambda  & 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3-\\lambda  & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -3-\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 & -1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-5-\\lambda )\\begin{vmatrix}3-\\lambda  & 0 & -2 \\\\ 7 & -3-\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ 5 & 0 & -1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, expanding the $3\\times 3$ determinant across the $2$nd column, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-5-\\lambda )\\cdot (-3-\\lambda )\\begin{vmatrix}3-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ 5 & -1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-5-\\lambda )\\cdot (-3-\\lambda )((3-\\lambda )(-1-\\lambda )+10) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +5)(\\lambda +3)({\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +7) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $D<0$ for the polynomial ${\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +7$, we have that the only real eigenvalues are $\\lambda =-5$ and $\\lambda =-3$ (both with algebraic multiplicity $1$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} :step/id #uuid "34fc937b-c4ca-51d0-b41f-cc48a4e031b8" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442277}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442275 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442272 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d0700ae-412d-51b3-a257-618c99674341" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\lambda \\in \\{-2,1,5\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442260} {:answer/id #uuid "481860b5-bb3d-58cb-94dc-529c4d401e99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\lambda \\in \\{-5,1,3\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442261} {:answer/id #uuid "558359c6-2d82-546e-a690-6c2a868d98cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\lambda \\in \\{1,2,5\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442262} {:answer/id #uuid "6819477a-eb82-5fe8-9144-a2c3b8ccd8c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\lambda \\in \\{-5,-1,2\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442263} {:answer/id #uuid "974ccec8-a09e-54db-b466-a20773981b7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\lambda \\in \\{-5,1,2\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442266}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442266} :answer-field/id #uuid "d8e6f41c-9e5c-55de-9517-8eaa2b14ab20" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442270}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "bedee111-5fec-56ad-a271-6904f3e057b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-140475" :question/problem "Determine all real eigenvalues $\\lambda $ of the matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the matrix $\\,A-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}3-\\lambda  & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3-\\lambda  & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -2-\\lambda \\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation is given by $det(A-\\lambda I)=0.$ \n\nExpanding the determinant across the $1$st row, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}3-\\lambda  & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3-\\lambda  & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -2-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )\\begin{vmatrix}-3-\\lambda  & 3 \\\\ 2 & -2-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix}-(-1)\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix}+(-1)\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -3-\\lambda  \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-{\\lambda }^{3}-2{\\lambda }^{2}+15\\lambda )+(-5-\\lambda )+(5+\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ -{\\lambda }^{3}-2{\\lambda }^{2}+13\\lambda -10 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{3}+2{\\lambda }^{2}-13\\lambda +10 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to solve this equation. We find the first root by testing some values. According to the rational zeros theorem, a list of good first guesses consists of all divisors of $10:$ \n\n$$\n\\pm 1,\\pm 2,\\pm 5,\\pm 10\n$$\n\nLet's pick $x=1.$ Substituting to the characteristic equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1)^{3}+2(1)^{2}-13(1)+10 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\\lambda =1$ is a root of the equation. So, we can factor $(\\lambda -1)$ out of ${\\lambda }^{3}+2{\\lambda }^{2}-13\\lambda +10$ using synthetic division:\n\n| $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $-13$ | $10$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $↓$ | $1$ | $3$ | $-10$ |\n|  | $1$ | $3$ | $-10$ | $0$ |\n\nSo, we obtain the following factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda -1)({\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda -10) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -1)(\\lambda +5)(\\lambda -2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-5,$ $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=1,\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{3}=2.$"} :step/id #uuid "41fe8de5-4b38-5378-88a7-29da8122c672" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442273}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442276 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125062 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "203333e3-42b8-50e5-ad7c-349f412c474d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448596} {:answer/id #uuid "909fc2e1-73fa-59b2-a46b-d44dc0bb0b16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448597}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186448596} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a9cdab4-a97f-58e7-b97a-81f65eb22730" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449338} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6017700f-5a71-5853-a457-72bd0d6622d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448598} {:answer/id #uuid "78230748-a3fa-5d9d-9caf-d1ef1fc40fb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448599}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186448599} :answer-field/id #uuid "baf3d724-150e-5574-a988-4d93412ca0c2" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449339} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f182a3b9-57f6-551f-b745-0c46637db13b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448600} {:answer/id #uuid "9f9693de-9b5c-55ce-8c6f-86871ae8c865" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448601}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186448601} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5f31ab0-0b07-530a-931a-fd795892e749" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449340}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c699a77c-6393-5572-b010-6743d9f3bb17" :question/math-academy-id "q-37355" :question/problem "$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -9 & -18 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -10 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -10 & 0 \\\\ -18 & 18 & -36 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $P$ given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\lambda =-1$ is an eigenvalue of $P$\n\n- **II.** $\\lambda =-10$ is an eigenvalue of $P$\n\n- **III.** The eigenvalue $\\lambda =-10$ has algebraic multiplicity $3$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the matrix $\\,P-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nP-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}-1-\\lambda  & -9 & -18 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -10-\\lambda  & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -10-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ -18 & 18 & -36 & -4-\\lambda \\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation is $det(P-\\lambda I)=0.$ \n\nNotice that we can expand the $4\\times 4$ determinant across the $2$nd row, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(P-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}-1-\\lambda  & -9 & -18 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -10-\\lambda  & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -10-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ -18 & 18 & -36 & -4-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-10-\\lambda )\\begin{vmatrix}-1-\\lambda  & -18 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -10-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ -18 & -36 & -4-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, expanding the $3\\times 3$ determinant across the $2$nd row, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-10-\\lambda )\\cdot (-10-\\lambda )\\begin{vmatrix}-1-\\lambda  & -3 \\\\ -18 & -4-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-10-\\lambda )^{2}((-1-\\lambda )(-4-\\lambda )-54) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +10)^{2}({\\lambda }^{2}+5\\lambda -50) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +10)^{2}(\\lambda +10)(\\lambda -5) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +10)^{3}(\\lambda -5) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the eigenvalues of $P$ are $\\lambda =-10$ (with algebraic multiplicity $3$) and $\\lambda =5.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "4407ab4d-760d-5929-a4aa-ceca8bb19594" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442274}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442277 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323910 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c92bdd9-561f-50f8-a0af-b026acb7e7d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\lambda }^{3}-4{\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -52=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321751} {:answer/id #uuid "679f1b6a-bb22-58ad-9008-20f76acaf14c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\lambda }^{3}-10{\\lambda }^{2}+43\\lambda -90=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321955} {:answer/id #uuid "688684d9-f540-59a5-826c-2faac539f6c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\lambda }^{3}+7{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda -48=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321961} {:answer/id #uuid "a6e445b9-3e7b-5df5-8de9-a07b0a91fd34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\lambda }^{3}-4{\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda +6=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322453} {:answer/id #uuid "c8f0d0d9-d2ec-529d-bfe9-31a08f8548aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\lambda }^{3}-4{\\lambda }^{2}+49\\lambda -138=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322711}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322453} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4874765-573f-531a-bb89-b09ddf9d2051" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323521}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4b421cae-1cc2-5e38-a509-6349aee206e1" :question/math-academy-id "q-37394" :question/problem "Find the characteristic equation of the matrix \n$B=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -8 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 8 & 3 \\\\ -6 & -10 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the matrix $\\,B-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nB-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}-1-\\lambda  & -8 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 8-\\lambda  & 3 \\\\ -6 & -10 & -3-\\lambda \\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation is $det(B-\\lambda I)=0.$ \n\nExpanding the determinant across the $1$st row, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}-1-\\lambda  & -8 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 8-\\lambda  & 3 \\\\ -6 & -10 & -3-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )\\begin{vmatrix}8-\\lambda  & 3 \\\\ -10 & -3-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix}-(-8)\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 3 \\\\ -6 & -3-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix}+(-4)\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 8-\\lambda  \\\\ -6 & -10\\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda +6)+8(-3\\lambda +9)-4(-6\\lambda +18) & =0 \\\\ (-{\\lambda }^{3}+4{\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda -6)+(-24\\lambda +72)-(-24\\lambda +72) & =0 \\\\ -{\\lambda }^{3}+4{\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda -6 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{3}-4{\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda +6 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "e50043bb-5fa2-5c1d-b766-cd5b3092a18e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442275}}] :activity/title "The Characteristic Equation of a Matrix" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442258}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492085} :activity/id #uuid "bb1cab8e-f559-528e-91c4-523487085390" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050376 :topic/id #uuid "44312a5e-e7e0-507f-86f6-c3bcdecfd7fe" :topic/math-academy-id 1964} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492076 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492037 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15237b61-8e4a-5f9d-a46d-f1c679f25f0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\lambda \\in \\{-1,-2,4\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491497} {:answer/id #uuid "b7d351e3-6f87-5e60-9b75-e0173bdd7a1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\lambda \\in \\{-1,1,-8\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491771} {:answer/id #uuid "cd09c6e2-8436-5664-8cd9-04b180376a9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\lambda \\in \\{1,8\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491802} {:answer/id #uuid "efa5985d-1ed5-5b85-9d5e-b97611697302" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\lambda \\in \\{-1,2,4\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491864} {:answer/id #uuid "f7df2b21-bb30-525d-9e2e-3424c69767ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\lambda \\in \\{-1,8\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491878}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491878} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b06b2ac-6eae-593d-9852-5522b16478ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491913}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "92a47338-e5c2-507b-a67f-16316e526b27" :question/math-academy-id "q-80821" :question/problem "Determine all real eigenvalues $\\lambda $ of the matrix \n$P=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the matrix $\\,P-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nP-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}3-\\lambda  & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 3-\\lambda \\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation is given by $det(P-\\lambda I)=0.$ \n\nExpanding the determinant across the $1$st row, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(P-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}3-\\lambda  & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 3-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )\\begin{vmatrix}-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix}-2\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 3-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix}+4\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -\\lambda  \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-{\\lambda }^{3}+6{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda -12)-2(-2-2\\lambda )+4(4+4\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ -{\\lambda }^{3}+6{\\lambda }^{2}+15\\lambda +8 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{3}-6{\\lambda }^{2}-15\\lambda -8 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to solve this equation. We find the first root by testing some values. According to the rational zeros theorem, a list of good first guesses consists of all divisors of $8:$ \n\n$$\n\\pm 1,\\pm 2,\\pm 4,\\pm 8\n$$\n\n- Let's pick $x=1.$ Substituting to the characteristic equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1)^{3}-6(1)^{2}-15(1)-8 & =-28.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\\lambda =1$ is not a root of the equation.\n\n- Let's pick $x=-1.$ Substituting to the characteristic equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)^{3}-6(-1)^{2}-15(-1)-8 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\\lambda =-1$ is a root of the equation.\n\nSo, we can factor $(\\lambda +1)$ out of ${\\lambda }^{3}-6{\\lambda }^{2}-15\\lambda -8$ using synthetic division:\n\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $-6$ | $-15$ | $-8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $↓$ | $-1$ | $7$ | $8$ |\n|  | $1$ | $-7$ | $-8$ | $0$ |\n\nSo, we obtain the following factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda +1)({\\lambda }^{2}-7\\lambda -8) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)(\\lambda +1)(\\lambda -8) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=8.$"} :step/id #uuid "09549905-c795-54bf-8c12-03fa23a953b9" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492106}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492085 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125013 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2dc50313-1349-57e5-bd5b-c32ef21846c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5{\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427375} {:answer/id #uuid "ba3bf449-9417-55d8-bda3-bcdf2a3abb0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\lambda }^{3}+2{\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda -2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427378} {:answer/id #uuid "bb4847f7-518c-5520-9f7b-8f055c77b9d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\lambda }^{3}-5{\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda =0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427379} {:answer/id #uuid "c9d9c2bc-05e4-5243-ab8a-17562b551bd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\lambda }^{3}-5{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda -2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427380} {:answer/id #uuid "f431214a-fe2c-58fb-acc6-9d70412cfbb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\lambda }^{3}-2\\lambda -2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427383}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427378} :answer-field/id #uuid "b55f74a2-418f-5fce-b67f-29118bc0950f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122678}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f39262c6-06ce-51e6-92a2-5f2acfb813a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-36824" :question/problem "Find the characteristic equation of the matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -5\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the matrix $\\,A-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\lambda  & -2 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 3-\\lambda  & 6 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -5-\\lambda \\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation is $det(A-\\lambda I)=0.$ \n\nExpanding the determinant across the $1$st row, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}-\\lambda  & -2 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 3-\\lambda  & 6 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -5-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ -\\lambda \\begin{vmatrix}3-\\lambda  & 6 \\\\ -2 & -5-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix}-(-2)\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 6 \\\\ 2 & -5-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix}+(-3)\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 3-\\lambda  \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ -\\lambda ({\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda -3)+2(2\\lambda -2)-3(2\\lambda -2) & =0 \\\\ (-{\\lambda }^{3}-2{\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda )+(4\\lambda -4)-(6\\lambda -6) & =0 \\\\ -{\\lambda }^{3}-2{\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda +2 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{3}+2{\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda -2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "2d974ac8-4b52-5e20-b579-6e1ce93781a6" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492087}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492087 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442271 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0069a32c-24a8-592d-aebc-701756d8bdcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442259} {:answer/id #uuid "8e52e58b-8a75-5be5-b33c-025f9395fdc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442264} {:answer/id #uuid "95f7c606-fabc-51e1-8528-9632cdd50f62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442265} {:answer/id #uuid "c80a4f86-8e3e-57b3-9a46-45e60e433bf6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442267} {:answer/id #uuid "f29dc51e-2c92-5c6d-8ac3-24dd5e961743" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442268}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442265} :answer-field/id #uuid "24804dcb-504d-588f-82a7-fb6fe2bd5e63" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442269}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "970b7afa-4ca1-5d4a-a4eb-b7279227d558" :question/math-academy-id "q-37356" :question/problem "$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $B$ given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\lambda =-5$ is an eigenvalue of $B$\n\nII. $\\lambda =-1$ is an eigenvalue of $B$\n\nIII. The eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3$ has algebraic multiplicity $2$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the matrix $\\,B-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nB-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}-5-\\lambda  & 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3-\\lambda  & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -3-\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 & -1-\\lambda \\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation is $det(B-\\lambda I)=0.$ \n\nNotice that we can expand the $4\\times 4$ determinant across the $1$st column, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}-5-\\lambda  & 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3-\\lambda  & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -3-\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 & -1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-5-\\lambda )\\begin{vmatrix}3-\\lambda  & 0 & -2 \\\\ 7 & -3-\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ 5 & 0 & -1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, expanding the $3\\times 3$ determinant across the $2$nd column, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-5-\\lambda )\\cdot (-3-\\lambda )\\begin{vmatrix}3-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ 5 & -1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-5-\\lambda )\\cdot (-3-\\lambda )((3-\\lambda )(-1-\\lambda )+10) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +5)(\\lambda +3)({\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +7) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $D<0$ for the polynomial ${\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +7$, we have that the only real eigenvalues are $\\lambda =-5$ and $\\lambda =-3$ (both with algebraic multiplicity $1$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} :step/id #uuid "340f24d2-fb9b-5c2f-b6ce-923f20f3fce5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492076}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492099 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272102 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44aa4cfa-3180-5442-8e75-ba1c05ae3cc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\lambda -1)(\\lambda +4)(\\lambda -8)(\\lambda +5)=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269895} {:answer/id #uuid "591dd63a-d199-5e93-bfbb-a40b87a92c10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\lambda -7)(\\lambda +4)(\\lambda +5)=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270057} {:answer/id #uuid "6e9bd84c-c861-54af-897d-8ef317304806" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\lambda -7)(\\lambda +4)(\\lambda -3)(\\lambda -5)=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270251} {:answer/id #uuid "8e737deb-7b2b-5c10-a2f1-ff581e3be55f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\lambda -7)(\\lambda +4)(\\lambda -3)(\\lambda +5)=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270503} {:answer/id #uuid "fa8c69b0-ffdb-53b4-9343-e90d90da6e9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\lambda +7)(\\lambda +4)(\\lambda -3)(\\lambda -2)=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271458}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186270503} :answer-field/id #uuid "95b21e22-4684-5abe-87a1-7e9e493eb9bd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271775}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "61624282-afa1-5aa5-87a3-f50541d7253a" :question/math-academy-id "q-140471" :question/problem "Find the characteristic equation of the matrix \n$P=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 6 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 0 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 11 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the matrix $\\,P-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nP-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}7-\\lambda  & 6 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4-\\lambda  & 0 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3-\\lambda  & 11 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -5-\\lambda \\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation is $det(P-\\lambda I)=0.$ \n\nSince the determinant of a triangular (or diagonal) matrix is given by the product of the entries on the main diagonal, we obtain the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(P-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}7-\\lambda  & 6 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4-\\lambda  & 0 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3-\\lambda  & 11 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -5-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (7-\\lambda )(-4-\\lambda )(3-\\lambda )(-5-\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -7)(\\lambda +4)(\\lambda -3)(\\lambda +5) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "95cae298-e2af-5367-a5a3-02954c8d6de6"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492106 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366518 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5fb7b1df-59af-5c98-b200-8e2db9ace225" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\lambda }^{3}-3{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda -10=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366520} {:answer/id #uuid "780e1a23-2b19-569c-80de-ba05aa572a33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\lambda }^{3}-4{\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda -6=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366521} {:answer/id #uuid "1e23239c-8a66-52b7-8ebb-bb679392ff42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\lambda }^{3}+4{\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda +10=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366522} {:answer/id #uuid "d9d592de-a8e2-59d7-af6e-a3bf5bfd67b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\lambda }^{3}-3{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda -6=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366523} {:answer/id #uuid "023efe6a-1a85-50a5-b630-83708ee21893" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\lambda }^{3}-4{\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda -10=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366524}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366524} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a1a646e-8c46-51ec-bc79-a886445b9b9d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366519}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "09a3b9db-e65e-598f-9745-d20fbdbcba69" :question/math-academy-id "q-86753" :question/problem "Find the characteristic equation of the matrix \n$R=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the matrix $\\,R-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nR-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}1-\\lambda  & 2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3-\\lambda \\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation is $det(R-\\lambda I)=0.$ \n\nExpanding the determinant across the $1$st column, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(R-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}1-\\lambda  & 2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )\\begin{vmatrix}-\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ 1 & 3-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix}-(-2)\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda -4)+2(-2\\lambda +7) & =0 \\\\ (-{\\lambda }^{3}+4{\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -4)+(-4\\lambda +14) & =0 \\\\ -{\\lambda }^{3}+4{\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda +10 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{3}-4{\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda -10 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "c5e318ec-24f1-53a2-b4da-1e201af09eaa" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492099}}] :activity/title "The Characteristic Equation of a Matrix" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491446}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 1500.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069899} :activity/id #uuid "afe6da6e-0855-538d-ac54-1e34f990246c" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050489 :topic/id #uuid "4d6f30e9-3dde-50ef-bfb2-830878d94eaf" :topic/math-academy-id 478} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069899 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186070016 :tutorial/content "We know how to integrate $\\cos{x}.$ But how do we integrate $\\cos{3x}?$ \n\nTo compute\n\n$$\n\\int \\cos{3x} \\, \\textrm dx,\n$$\n\nwe can use substitution. If we let $u=3x,$ then\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = 3\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm d x = \\dfrac 1 3 \\textrm d u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** After solving an integral using substitution, we should always double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+C) & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3x)+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "075eaa34-761d-56ce-92f5-b498b52b92ac" :tutorial/math-academy-id 854 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "9a303692-65c8-5fbf-8f20-8a7e6213e724" :step/math-academy-id 617129 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069900}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069900 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068915 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068567 :question/id #uuid "0021ae6a-8ef8-564e-9c2e-fb5f9e8f7313" :question/math-academy-id "e-464"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "746d1bc6-98ec-59a1-bec6-9dcfb7081a5d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048332} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048777}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482725} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069047} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069149} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237798} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237799} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319064} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319362} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186429932} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482716} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482718} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482722} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482724}] :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Trigonometric Functions With Linear Arguments"} :step/id #uuid "2fa264e5-2795-5ecb-81bd-f24706045bc7" :step/math-academy-id 617130 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069901}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069901 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068916 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068568 :question/id #uuid "daf5cc74-4c7d-500a-9345-03bd6b73a333" :question/math-academy-id "e-465"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "084eb4cc-2f84-5288-90a6-be84066922b6" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069004} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069006} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250146} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269130} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290366} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319094} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319363} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319364} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186429931} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186429933}] :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Reciprocal Trigonometric Functions With Linear Arguments"} :step/id #uuid "06591b79-5220-5187-8baa-3fb3da419824" :step/math-academy-id 617133 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069902}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069902 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186070017 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we want to calculate $\\displaystyle \\int 2x \\cos{(x^2)}\\,\\textrm d x.$\n\nNotice that if we let $u = x^2,$ then we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\underbrace{2x}_{u'} \\cos({\\overbrace{x^2}^u})\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nIn other words, the factor multiplying the trigonometric function is the derivative of the argument! Integrals like this can always be solved using substitution.\n\nSo, let $u=x^2.$ Differentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d} u}{\\textrm{d}x } = 2x \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm d u = 2x\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we now substitute and evaluate, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})\\cdot 2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2})+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f981f86c-ae3e-5137-b347-764c79c6eb45" :tutorial/math-academy-id 857 :tutorial/title "Integrating Products Containing Trigonometric Functions Using Substitution"} :step/id #uuid "ac0f9fbe-d73f-5211-9b09-02cf5b0b4700" :step/math-academy-id 617136 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069903}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069903 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068917 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068569 :question/id #uuid "234c7a86-037c-5316-9419-14db90d1da60" :question/math-academy-id "e-466"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d359337c-4ec2-5100-bdac-d0858619db3b" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486563} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069045} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069046} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269003} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269033} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269045} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297287} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319366} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319367} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486542} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486552} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486555}] :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Products Containing Trigonometric Functions Using Substitution"} :step/id #uuid "49d49ff9-a1d0-509a-8981-81289b3ebc93" :step/math-academy-id 617137 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069904}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069904 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068918 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068570 :question/id #uuid "03e461a3-0fe0-5fb5-813e-ae23a5dc606a" :question/math-academy-id "e-467"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc24eade-1b3d-5672-88fd-0bb48b371509" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487553} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069003} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069378} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237790} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250148} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320749} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320750} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320751} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342058} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186429930} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487529} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487530} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487536} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487541} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487549}] :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Quotients Containing Trigonometric Functions Using Substitution"} :step/id #uuid "590cad06-7d45-5569-8593-720ef7930553" :step/math-academy-id 617140}] :activity/title "Integrating Trigonometric Functions Using Substitution" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186069001}]}
